const e=[{id:1,icon:"FireExtinguisher",accent:"#dc2626",title:"Fire extinguishers",subtitle:"The PASS technique and types of extinguishers",summary:"The fire extinguisher is a key piece of equipment in civil defense. This lesson covers: types of extinguishers (water, foam, CO₂, dry powder, wet chemical and clean agent), fire classes according to the NFPA system (A, B, C, D, K), the PASS technique, monthly inspection and annual servicing, safe use and real-life scenarios. This information is for middle and high school students, so that they understand how trained adults use extinguishers. In a real fire, a student warns others, gets out and calls 115.",blocks:[{heading:"Types of extinguishers and their uses",body:`• Water extinguisher: for Class A fires (ordinary materials such as wood, paper and fabric). Water removes heat. Never use it on electrical or oil fires!
• Foam extinguisher (AFFF): for Class A and B (flammable liquids). Foam forms a layer over the fire and cuts off the oxygen. AFFF contains PFAS chemicals and is being restricted in many countries; fluorine-free foam (F3) is replacing it.
• CO₂ extinguisher: for Class B and C (liquids and electrical equipment). CO₂ cuts the fire off from oxygen and leaves no residue; it is best for electronic equipment and laboratories. CO₂ has a short range and is not for deep-seated wood or paper fires. Hold only the handle, never the horn, which gets very cold. In a small room, leave and ventilate after use, because CO₂ pushes out the oxygen.
• Dry powder extinguisher (dry chemical, ABC): for Class A, B and C. It is the most common type. The chemical powder stops the chemical reaction of the fire. Note: ABC powder is not suitable for Class D (metals) or Class K (cooking oil).
• Wet chemical extinguisher: specifically for Class K (kitchen fires and frying oil). It turns the oil into a soap-like substance (saponification) and forms a protective layer over it.
• Clean agent extinguisher: for sensitive equipment such as computers and servers. It leaves no residue and does not damage the equipment.`,image:"media/images/ext_types_all.png"},{heading:"The PASS technique (step by step)",body:`This is how trained adults use an extinguisher. Students do not fight fires: they warn others, get out and call 115.

Before you use an extinguisher (every answer must be yes):
• The fire is small and just starting, like a fire in a wastebasket or a pan.
• The extinguisher is the right type for this fire.
• Your exit is behind you and the fire is not between you and the exit.
• The alarm has been raised: others are warned and 115 has been called.
• You are trained to use the extinguisher.
• No smoke or heat is blocking your way out.
• If the fire does not go out at once, stop, get out and close the door behind you.

P = Pull: pull out the safety pin. This pin locks the extinguisher handle.

A = Aim: aim the nozzle at the base of the fire, not at the top of the flames! If you aim at the top of the fire, it will not go out and it may spread.

S = Squeeze: squeeze the handle slowly and firmly. If you let go of the handle, the agent stops; this way you stay in control.

S = Sweep: sweep the nozzle from side to side at the base of the fire until the fire is completely out. If the fire flares up again, repeat PASS.

Safe distance: start from the distance shown on the extinguisher label or in the manufacturer's instructions, with your exit behind you, and move closer only as the fire gets smaller.

PASS is the general method for most hand-held extinguishers. Class D, wet chemical, clean agent and wheeled units: follow the label and the manufacturer's instructions.`,image:"media/images/pass_detailed.png"},{heading:"Monthly inspection and annual servicing",body:`Monthly inspection (visual):
• Location: the extinguisher is in its place, clearly visible, mounted on the wall and near a door; its top is no higher than 1.5 meters above the floor (NFPA 10)
• Pressure gauge, if there is one: the needle is in the green zone. CO₂ extinguishers have no gauge: lift one to feel that it is full
• Safety pin and plastic seal: in place and unbroken
• Label: readable and facing out
• Nozzle and hose: no breaks, no cracks and no blockages
• Extinguisher body: no rust, no dents, no leaks and no damage
• Service tag: shows the date of the last annual service

Annual servicing and recharging:
• Inspection and maintenance: a qualified technician inspects and services the extinguishers every year
• Recharge: after every use (even a short one), a qualified technician must recharge the extinguisher immediately
• 6-year maintenance: stored-pressure dry chemical extinguishers that need a 12-year hydrostatic test are emptied and checked inside every 6 years
• Hydrostatic test (pressure test of the cylinder), by type (NFPA 10): every 5 years for water, foam (AFFF), CO₂ and stainless-steel dry chemical extinguishers; every 12 years for stored-pressure dry chemical extinguishers with mild steel, brazed brass or aluminum shells. This is a test, not a replacement of the extinguisher. The rules for Duhok are not yet confirmed`,image:"media/images/ext_inspection.png"}],examples:["The ABC extinguisher is the most common type, for homes and schools.","CO₂ for laboratories and electronic equipment.","Wet chemical is specifically for kitchen fires (K).","Distance in the PASS technique: start from the distance on the extinguisher label, with your exit behind you.","Monthly inspection: pin + seal + label + hose + service tag + gauge (if there is one).","Recharge after every use; professional servicing every year.","Always keep your exit route behind you.","For a home, adults choose an extinguisher they can lift and use; the weight is on the label."],tips:["Color is not the fire class. Under the European standard BS EN 3 the extinguisher body is red; in the UK a colored band (BS 7863) shows the agent: cream = foam, blue = dry powder, black = CO₂, yellow = wet chemical; water has no band. Other countries use other colors and the color practice in Duhok is not yet confirmed, so always read the label and its class symbols.",'First the "Before you use an extinguisher" check (every answer must be yes), then PASS: Pull, Aim, Squeeze, Sweep.',"Distance: start from the distance on the extinguisher label, with your exit behind you, and move closer only as the fire gets smaller.","Always keep your exit route behind you.","If the fire does not get smaller in the first few seconds, or the extinguisher runs empty, get out immediately and call 115."],warnings:["Never use a water extinguisher on electrical fires (C) or hot oil fires (K)! Water conducts electricity and spreads hot oil violently. Use an extinguisher only on small fires in their early stage; do not use it if the fire is bigger than a wastebasket, is spreading or smoke is building up: get out immediately and call 115!"],realCase:"In a kitchen in Duhok, frying oil caught fire. A trained staff member used a wet chemical (K) extinguisher, following the instructions on its label, and put out the fire. If a water extinguisher had been used, the fire would have spread violently and caused a major disaster. Knowing the types of extinguishers saves lives!",video:"media/videos/lesson_extinguisher_full.mp4",game:{instruction:"Which fire class is each type of extinguisher for? Choose the correct ones:",items:[{text:"CO₂ extinguisher → electrical fire (C)",emoji:"🔵",correct:!0},{text:"Water extinguisher → kitchen oil fire (K)",emoji:"💧",correct:!1},{text:"ABC extinguisher → Class A, B and C fires",emoji:"🔴",correct:!0},{text:"Foam extinguisher → metal fire (D)",emoji:"🟡",correct:!1}]},activity:{prompt:"You are in the chemistry laboratory at school. Suddenly a fire starts on a bench: a flammable organic liquid (Class B) has caught fire, and it is close to electrical equipment (Class C). There are two extinguishers there: a water extinguisher and a CO₂ extinguisher. What will you do?",image:"media/images/activity_lab_fire.png",options:[{text:'I shout "Fire!" to warn the teacher and my classmates, we leave the lab by the nearest exit and close the door behind us, and I call 115 from outside. Only the teacher or trained staff decide whether to use the CO₂ extinguisher on a small fire.',correct:!0,feedback:"This is the student's job: warn others, get out, close the door and call 115; trained staff decide about the extinguisher."},{text:"I use the water extinguisher on the fire",correct:!1,feedback:"Water is wrong for burning liquids and conducts electricity, and students do not fight fires: warn others, get out and call 115."},{text:"I use the CO₂ extinguisher on the fire myself",correct:!1,feedback:"Students do not fight fires. Warn others, get out and call 115."},{text:"I leave on my own without alerting the teacher or my classmates",correct:!1,feedback:'Getting out is right, but others must be warned too: shout "Fire!", help everyone leave and call 115 from outside.'}]},quiz:[{question:"What does PASS stand for?",options:[{text:"Pull, Aim, Squeeze, Sweep",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct! Pull the pin, aim at the base, squeeze the handle, sweep from side to side."},{text:"Push, Aim, Spray, Stop",correct:!1,feedback:"PASS starts with Pull (the safety pin) and ends with Sweep; Push, Spray and Stop are not PASS steps."},{text:"Pull, Attack, Spray, Sweep",correct:!1,feedback:"A means Aim at the base of the fire, and the first S means Squeeze the handle, not Attack or Spray."}],explanation:"PASS means Pull the pin, Aim at the base, Squeeze the handle, Sweep side to side. Trained adults use it only when every pre-use answer is yes; students warn others, get out and call 115."},{question:"Which extinguisher is best for electrical fires (C)?",options:[{text:"CO₂",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct! CO₂ leaves no residue and does not conduct electricity."},{text:"Water",correct:!1,feedback:"No! Water conducts electricity."},{text:"Wet chemical (K)",correct:!1,feedback:"No! That is for kitchen fires."}]},{question:"What is a wet chemical extinguisher specifically for?",options:[{text:"Frying oil and kitchen fires",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct! It turns the oil into a soap-like substance and smothers the fire."},{text:"Ordinary fires such as wood fires",correct:!1,feedback:"Wood and paper fires are Class A, for water, foam or ABC powder; wet chemical is made for cooking oil (K)."},{text:"Metal fires (D)",correct:!1,feedback:"No! Class D needs an agent whose label lists that metal."}],explanation:"Wet chemical is made for Class K: it turns burning cooking oil into a soap-like substance and forms a layer over it. Ordinary (A) and metal (D) fires need other agents."},{question:"In the PASS technique, where do you find how far from the fire to stand?",options:[{text:"On the extinguisher label or in the manufacturer's instructions",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct! Start from that distance with your exit behind you, and move closer only as the fire gets smaller."},{text:"One fixed distance is used for every extinguisher, whatever its size",correct:!1,feedback:"No. The distance depends on the type and size of the extinguisher; it is on the label."},{text:"As close as possible, so that the powder reaches the flames",correct:!1,feedback:"No, that is dangerous!"}]},{question:"When should extinguishers be inspected?",options:[{text:"Every month by eye and every year by a qualified technician",correct:!0,feedback:"A monthly visual check finds problems early, and a qualified technician inspects and services each extinguisher every year."},{text:"Only after they have been used, so that they can be refilled",correct:!1,feedback:"An extinguisher must be ready before a fire, so it is checked every month; after any use it is recharged at once."},{text:"Once every 5 years, when a qualified technician tests them",correct:!1,feedback:"No! That is the hydrostatic test interval for some types, not the routine inspection."}],explanation:"Extinguishers get a visual check every month and an inspection and service by a qualified technician every year. The hydrostatic test every 5 or 12 years, by type, is a separate test."},{question:"You are trained and an ABC extinguisher is next to you. Which situation means you must not use it and must get out?",options:[{text:"Smoke is gathering and the fire is between you and the door",correct:!0},{text:"The fire is in a wastebasket and has only just started",correct:!1},{text:"The label shows that the extinguisher suits this type of fire",correct:!1},{text:"Others are warned and someone has already called 115",correct:!1}],explanation:"Every check answer must be yes. If smoke builds up or the fire is between you and the exit, do not use the extinguisher: get out, close the door and call 115 from outside."}],steps:[{title:"Raise the alarm first",detail:'Shout "Fire!" so that everyone hears, tell a teacher or another adult and make sure someone calls 115. Warning people comes before any extinguisher.',critical:!0},{title:"Answer the check questions",detail:"Before anyone uses an extinguisher, every answer must be yes: the fire is small and just starting; the label shows that the extinguisher suits this fire; the exit is behind you; others are warned and 115 is called; the person is trained; no smoke or heat blocks the way out. If any answer is no, get out and close the door.",critical:!0},{title:"PASS only for a trained person",detail:"Only a trained person, and only when every answer is yes: Pull the safety pin, Aim the nozzle at the base of the fire, Squeeze the handle and Sweep from side to side. Start from the distance on the label, with the exit behind you."},{title:"If it does not go out at once, get out",detail:"If the fire does not get smaller at once or the extinguisher runs empty, stop, get out, close the door behind you and call 115 from outside. A used extinguisher is recharged by a qualified technician.",critical:!0}],caseStudy:[{prompt:"During a break, paper in your classroom wastebasket catches fire. The flames are small and there is only a little smoke. An ABC extinguisher hangs by the door, and you had extinguisher training last month. What do you do first?",options:[{text:'I shout "Fire!", send a classmate to the teacher to make sure 115 is called and check that the door is behind me',correct:!0,feedback:"Raising the alarm comes first; only then can every check answer be yes, with the exit behind you."},{text:"I grab the extinguisher at once and spray the top of the flames before the fire grows",correct:!1,feedback:"Skipping the alarm and the check is dangerous, and aiming at the top of the flames does not put the fire out: aim at the base."},{text:"I open all the windows wide so that the smoke can get out of the classroom",correct:!1,feedback:"Opening the windows feeds the fire with oxygen and wastes time; raise the alarm and keep the way out clear."},{text:"I carry the burning wastebasket out into the corridor to get it away from us",correct:!1,feedback:"Carrying a burning basket can burn you and spread the fire to the corridor; leave it, raise the alarm and keep the exit behind you."}]},{prompt:"The alarm is raised and the teacher is on the way. In those few seconds the fire has spread to the curtain and smoke is gathering under the ceiling. What do you do now?",options:[{text:"I leave the extinguisher, we all get out, close the door and go to the assembly point; 115 is told from outside",correct:!0,feedback:"The fire is no longer small and smoke is building up, so the check now says no: get out, close the door and call 115 from outside."},{text:"I use PASS from close up, because the ABC extinguisher suits paper and curtains",correct:!1,feedback:"The right type is not enough: the fire is spreading and smoke is gathering, so at least one answer is no. Get out."},{text:"I go back to my desk for my bag and phone, then I leave with the others",correct:!1,feedback:"Going back for things costs seconds you may not have in smoke; leave everything and get out."},{text:"I open the door wide and stand in the doorway to watch whether the fire grows",correct:!1,feedback:"An open door lets smoke and fire spread; get out, close the door behind you and go to the assembly point."}]}]},{id:2,icon:"FlameKindling",accent:"#ea580c",title:"Fire Classes A-B-C-D-K",subtitle:"Recognizing types of fire and extinguishing methods",summary:"Not all fires are the same. Each type of fire needs a different method. Knowing the fire classes is the foundation of every firefighter's work. This lesson uses the letters of the American NFPA 10 system (A, B, C, D, K) as the project reference; the official system for Duhok is not yet confirmed. In the European EN 2 system the letters are different: NFPA A = EN A; NFPA B (liquids) = EN B; NFPA B (gases) = EN C; NFPA C (live electrical equipment) has no EN class; NFPA D = EN D; NFPA K = EN F. If you use the wrong method, the fire will get worse.",blocks:[{heading:"Class A: ordinary materials",body:`• What it is: wood, paper, fabric, plastic; ordinary solid materials that burn
• Method: water (cooling), foam, ABC powder
• Symbol (NFPA): a green triangle with the letter A
• Examples: house fires, trash bins, curtains`,image:"media/images/class_a.png"},{heading:"Classes B, C, D and K (NFPA)",body:`• B: flammable liquids and gases: gasoline, oil, alcohol, thinner, gas. Liquids: foam, CO₂ or ABC powder; for alcohol and other polar solvents: CO₂, ABC powder or alcohol-resistant (AR) foam, because ordinary foam breaks down on them. Gas: stop the gas first if it is safe; never put out a gas flame you cannot stop. Never use water!
• C: live electrical equipment: wiring, appliances and electrical panels. First, if you can do so safely, cut off the electricity. Method: CO₂, ABC powder.
• D: combustible metals: magnesium, titanium, sodium. Method: only an extinguisher or agent whose label lists that metal; the agent differs from metal to metal. Never use water, ordinary powder or foam!
• K: hot cooking oil. Method: wet chemical, or put a lid on the pan and turn off the heat under the pan. Water is a disaster and ABC powder is not enough!`,image:"media/images/class_bcdk.png"},{heading:"The fire triangle and extinguishing methods",body:`Fire is made up of three things: heat + fuel + oxygen (O₂). If one of them is removed, the fire goes out. A fourth element, the chemical chain reaction, makes the fire tetrahedron; ABC powder works mainly by stopping it.
• Cooling: water lowers the heat of the fire → Class A
• Smothering: foam or CO₂ cut the fire off from oxygen → Class B
• Removing the fuel: move the fuel away from the fire, for example by closing the gas valve
• Stopping the chain reaction: chemical powder stops the chemical reaction of the fire → ABC powder`,image:"media/images/fire_triangle_adv.png"}],examples:["A = wood and paper → water.","B = gasoline → foam or CO₂; gas → stop the gas first, if it is safe.","C = electricity → CO₂.","D = metals → only an agent whose label lists that metal.","K = hot oil → wet chemical.","Water + hot oil = explosion and spreading fire!","Water + electricity = electric shock!","Fire triangle: heat + fuel + O₂."],tips:["Learn to recognize the fire classes by their symbols.","Water is only for Class A.","CO₂ is for Class B and C.","Wet chemical is for Class K."],warnings:["Never pour water on an oil fire (K)! Under hot oil, water instantly turns to steam, throws the burning oil violently upward and a large fire spreads quickly. This is extremely dangerous."],realCase:"At a factory in Erbil, a metal fire (Class D, magnesium) broke out. A worker tried to put it out with water, but the fire grew violently larger! A specialist then used a Class D powder whose label lists magnesium and put the fire out. Knowing the fire classes saves lives!",video:"media/videos/lesson_fire_classes.mp4",game:{instruction:"Match each fire class with its correct extinguishing method:",items:[{text:"Hot oil (K) → wet chemical",emoji:"🍳",correct:!0},{text:"Paper (A) → water",emoji:"📄",correct:!0},{text:"Gasoline (B) → foam or CO₂",emoji:"⛽",correct:!0},{text:"Electricity (C) → water",emoji:"⚡",correct:!1}]},activity:{prompt:"Frying oil has caught fire in the school kitchen. There is a water extinguisher and a wet chemical (K) extinguisher there. What will you do?",image:"media/images/activity_kitchen_fire.png",options:[{text:'I keep away from the stove, shout "Fire!" to warn the kitchen staff and a teacher, get out and call 115. A trained adult turns off the stove and puts a lid on the pan, or uses the wet chemical (K) extinguisher.',correct:!0,feedback:"Hot oil fires are for trained adults: keeping away, raising the alarm, getting out and calling 115 is the student's job."},{text:"I pour water onto the hot oil",correct:!1,feedback:"Water under hot oil turns to steam at once and throws burning oil upward; never pour water on an oil fire."},{text:"I go to the pan and use the wet chemical (K) extinguisher myself",correct:!1,feedback:"Students do not go near a burning pan; this is a job for trained adults. Get out and call 115."},{text:"I pick up the burning pan and carry it outside",correct:!1,feedback:"Moving a burning pan can splash burning oil and spread the fire; keep away from the stove, warn others and call 115."}]},quiz:[{question:"What happens if water is poured onto an oil fire (K)?",options:[{text:"An explosion and the fire spreads",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct! The water turns to steam and spreads the burning oil."},{text:"The fire goes out as the oil cools",correct:!1,feedback:"Water does not put out an oil fire: it turns to steam under the oil and spreads the flames violently."},{text:"The fire stays the same size",correct:!1,feedback:"Something dangerous happens: the water turns to steam at once and throws burning oil upward, spreading the fire."}],explanation:"Under hot oil, water instantly turns to steam and throws the burning oil upward, so the fire spreads fast. Never pour water on a Class K fire."},{question:"In a Class C (electrical) fire, what is the first step?",options:[{text:"Cut off the electricity, if it is safe",correct:!0,feedback:"Right: cut the power first if it is safe; then warn others, get out and call 115."},{text:"Pour water on the equipment at once",correct:!1,feedback:"Water conducts electricity, so pouring it on live equipment can cause an electric shock."},{text:"Wait for the fire to go out all by itself",correct:!1,feedback:"Doing nothing lets the fire grow: warn others, get out and call 115."}],explanation:"In a Class C fire the equipment is live, so the first step is to cut off the electricity if it is safe; never use water. Students warn others, get out and call 115."},{question:"What is the fire triangle made up of?",options:[{text:"Heat + fuel + oxygen",correct:!0,feedback:"These three together make fire; removing any one of them, by cooling, smothering or removing the fuel, puts it out."},{text:"Water + wind + heat",correct:!1,feedback:"Water and wind are not sides of the triangle; the three sides are heat, fuel and oxygen."},{text:"Smoke + fire + oxygen",correct:!1,feedback:"Smoke and fire are not sides of the triangle; the three sides are heat, fuel and oxygen."}],explanation:"Fire needs heat, fuel and oxygen together; if one of them is removed, the fire goes out. This is why cooling, smothering and removing the fuel work."},{question:"At a picnic, the hose of a gas cylinder catches fire and nobody can reach the valve safely. What is right?",options:[{text:"Do not put out the flame; get everyone away and call 115",correct:!0},{text:"Throw a wet blanket over the flame at once to put it out",correct:!1},{text:"Spray the flame with water until it goes out completely",correct:!1}],explanation:"If the gas cannot be stopped, putting out the flame lets unburned gas build up, and it can explode. Get everyone away and call 115; a qualified person stops the gas only if it is safe."},{question:"Which pair correctly matches a fire to a suitable method?",options:[{text:"Burning paper in a bin (A): water or ABC powder",correct:!0},{text:"Burning cooking oil (K): ABC powder is enough",correct:!1},{text:"Burning magnesium (D): water or ordinary foam",correct:!1},{text:"Live electrical panel (C): a water extinguisher",correct:!1}],explanation:"Class A materials such as paper can be cooled with water or ABC powder. Cooking oil needs wet chemical or a lid, metals need an agent whose label lists that metal, and water is never used on live electrical equipment."}],steps:[{title:"Find out what is burning",detail:"Wood, paper or fabric is Class A; a liquid or gas is B; live electrical equipment is C; a metal is D; cooking oil is K. Read the symbols on the extinguisher label: color is never the class."},{title:"Never use the wrong agent",detail:"No water on cooking oil (K), live electrical equipment (C) or metals (D). Never put out a gas flame that cannot be stopped: unburned gas builds up and can explode.",critical:!0},{title:"Warn others, get out and call 115",detail:'Shout "Fire!", get everyone out, close the door behind you and call 115 from outside. Trained adults decide about cutting the power, stopping the gas or using an extinguisher, and only if it is safe.',critical:!0},{title:"Tell the firefighters what is burning",detail:"When you call 115 or the team arrives, say what is burning: oil in a pan, an electrical device, a gas cylinder or a metal. This helps them choose the right method."}],caseStudy:[{prompt:"At home in the evening, a power strip behind the television starts to smoke and a small flame appears. Your younger brother runs to the kitchen to fill a glass of water. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I stop him, because water on live equipment can give an electric shock, and I warn the adults at once",correct:!0,feedback:"This is a Class C fire: water conducts electricity. Stop the water and raise the alarm; an adult cuts the power if it is safe."},{text:"I throw the water on it quickly myself while the flame is still small",correct:!1,feedback:"Even a small electrical fire must never get water: it conducts electricity and can give you a shock."},{text:"I pull the plug of the burning power strip out of the wall with my hand",correct:!1,feedback:"Touching burning, live equipment can burn you or give you a shock; warn an adult, who cuts the power at the breaker if it is safe."},{text:"I open the window and wait to see whether the flame goes out by itself",correct:!1,feedback:"Waiting lets the fire grow and fresh air feeds it; raise the alarm at once."}]},{prompt:"Your father has cut the power at the breaker, but now the curtain beside the television is burning and smoke is filling the room. What do you do?",options:[{text:"We all leave the house, close the doors behind us and call 115 from outside, saying it started at an electrical device",correct:!0,feedback:"The fire has grown and smoke is spreading, so it is no longer a small fire: get out, close the doors and tell 115 what is burning."},{text:"Now that the power is off, I throw a bucket of water on the curtain myself",correct:!1,feedback:"The fire is spreading and smoke fills the room, so it is no longer a fire for anyone at home to fight: get out and call 115."},{text:"I stay low in the room with a wet cloth over my mouth and wait for 115",correct:!1,feedback:"Staying in a smoke-filled room is dangerous; get out while the way is clear and call 115 from outside."},{text:"I go round the other rooms first to collect the phones and documents",correct:!1,feedback:"Smoke spreads fast, and collecting things takes time you may not have. Get everyone out and call 115."}]}]},{id:3,icon:"Activity",accent:"#d97706",title:"Earthquakes: advanced level",subtitle:"Building safety and rescue",summary:'At the advanced level, we do not only learn "Drop, Cover, Hold On"; we also learn how buildings are built, how to check for damage after an earthquake, the principles of rescuing people trapped under rubble and how to support rescue teams.',blocks:[{heading:"Construction and structural safety",body:`• Buildings constructed without earthquake engineering (Seismic Engineering) are the most dangerous
• Weak points: an open ground floor (soft story), old columns, foundations without concrete
• The Kurdistan Region lies on the Zagros mountain belt, which is a seismically active zone; therefore earthquakes can happen here
• During the shaking: Drop to the ground, take Cover under a sturdy table and Hold On (Drop, Cover, Hold On), away from windows and heavy equipment. Do not go into a doorway and do not trust the "triangle of life"
• After an earthquake, inspect buildings: cracks in walls, leaning columns, damage to foundations`,image:"media/images/seismic_building.png"},{heading:"Rescue from rubble (urban SAR)",body:`• Rescuing people from under rubble is the job of trained Civil Defense teams (115). Students never climb onto rubble or go into damaged buildings, even with gloves and a helmet: rubble can shift and collapse
• From a safe place, listen and call out, note where a voice comes from and tell the rescue team
• Keep other people away and follow the rescue team's instructions
• Be aware: gas, electricity and water can become hazards
• For information: rescue teams sort casualties by color (Triage): red = urgent, yellow = can wait, green = minor injury, black = dead or beyond help. Students do not do this; they report and help only when the team asks`,image:"media/images/urban_sar.png"},{heading:"After the shaking: utilities, damaged buildings and aftershocks",body:`• Expect aftershocks: if the ground shakes again, Drop, Cover, Hold On again
• Check yourself and the people around you for injuries; if people are trapped or injured, call 115 and 122
• If you smell gas or hear it leaking: do not strike matches or lighters and do not touch any switch; close the gas valve only if it is close and you can reach it safely, without going into the area where the gas has collected; otherwise do not go near it. Get out, warn others and call 115 from outside
• If you are in a damaged building, get out and move away from it; do not go back in until specialists have checked it
• Stay away from fallen power lines and anything touching them, and call 115
• Listen to official announcements on a battery-powered radio, television or your phone, and follow the instructions of Civil Defense
• For the emergency kit, see the lesson "The emergency kit" in Community awareness`,image:"media/images/eq_after_shaking.png"}],examples:["Building inspection: cracks, columns, foundations.","Rescue: listen from a safe place and report; never climb onto rubble.","Emergency kit: water + food + medicine + flashlight.","Close the gas valve only if you smell gas or hear it leaking, and only if you can reach it safely.","Use a radio to listen for warnings.","Triage (rescue teams): red, yellow, green, black.","Be prepared for 72 hours.","Agree on an assembly point."],tips:["Always keep your emergency kit ready.","During the shaking: Drop, Cover, Hold On.","Do not use the elevator.","Stay outside until specialists have inspected the building."],warnings:["After the first earthquake, always expect aftershocks. Do not go into damaged buildings! If you smell gas or hear it leaking: do not touch any switch or flame, close the gas valve only if it is close and you can reach it safely, then get out and call 115 from outside. Do not use tap water for drinking until it has been tested."],realCase:"On November 12, 2017, a strong earthquake (Mw 7.3, according to the USGS) struck near the Iran–Iraq border, south of Halabja. In an earthquake like this, trained volunteers can help the rescue teams: they know how to listen for the voices of people trapped under rubble and how to sort casualties using the Triage system. Knowledge saves lives!",video:"media/videos/lesson_earthquake_adv.mp4",game:{instruction:"After an earthquake, which actions are correct?",items:[{text:"If you smell gas, get out and call 115 from outside",emoji:"🚪",correct:!0},{text:"Go into the damaged building",emoji:"🏠",correct:!1},{text:"Tell the rescue team where you heard a voice",emoji:"📢",correct:!0},{text:"Use the elevator",emoji:"🛗",correct:!1}]},activity:{prompt:"An earthquake of magnitude 6.2 (on the Richter scale) has struck. You are at school. A nearby building has cracked and you can hear someone's voice coming from under the rubble. What will you do?",image:"media/images/activity_eq_adv.png",options:[{text:"I immediately alert the teacher and we call 115 and 122. I mark where the voice is coming from and talk loudly to the injured person, but I do not go into the cracked building until the rescue teams arrive",correct:!0,feedback:"Alerting the teacher, calling 115 and 122 and marking the voice for rescuers helps most; only trained teams go into damaged buildings."},{text:"I go into the cracked building on my own",correct:!1,feedback:"Rubble can shift and collapse; students never go into damaged buildings, even with gloves and a helmet. Report the voice to rescuers."},{text:"I do nothing and just wait",correct:!1,feedback:"Someone trapped needs help fast: alert the teacher, call 115 and 122, and tell the rescue team where the voice is."},{text:"I run home",correct:!1,feedback:"Running away leaves the trapped person without help; stay in a safe place, alert the teacher and report the voice to rescuers."}]},quiz:[{question:"What does Triage mean?",options:[{text:"Sorting casualties by the priority and urgency of their condition",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct! Rescue teams use red = urgent, yellow = can wait, green = minor, black = dead or beyond help."},{text:"Giving first aid to each casualty in the order in which they arrive",correct:!1,feedback:"Triage is not treatment: it means sorting casualties by the priority and urgency of their condition."},{text:"Getting everyone out of the building by the nearest safe exit",correct:!1,feedback:"Triage is not about leaving a building: it means rescue teams sort casualties by urgency, using colors."}],explanation:"Triage means rescue teams sort casualties by how urgent their condition is, using colors: red, yellow, green and black. Students do not triage; they report and help only when the team asks."},{question:"After an earthquake, when do you close the gas valve?",options:[{text:"Only if you smell or hear gas and can reach the valve safely",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct! Then get out and call 115 from outside."},{text:"Every time, before anything else, even if there is no smell",correct:!1,feedback:"No! Only if there are signs of a gas leak."},{text:"Never, even if you smell gas and the valve is right beside you",correct:!1,feedback:"No! If you smell gas and the valve can be reached safely, close it; if not, do not go near it: get out and call 115 from outside."}]},{question:"After the shaking, you are inside a cracked, damaged building. What should you do?",options:[{text:"Get out, move away from the building and stay out until specialists have checked it",correct:!0},{text:"Stay inside near a wall until the aftershocks stop, then decide whether to leave",correct:!1},{text:"Go back in quickly to collect your bag and your documents, then wait outside for news",correct:!1}],explanation:"Damaged buildings can collapse, especially in aftershocks. Get out, move away and do not go back in until specialists have checked the building."},{question:"Strong shaking starts while you are in class. Which is the safest place?",options:[{text:"Under a sturdy desk, away from the windows, holding on",correct:!0},{text:"In the doorway of the classroom, holding the door frame",correct:!1},{text:'Beside a big cupboard, in the "triangle of life"',correct:!1},{text:"On the stairs, on the way down to the playground",correct:!1}],explanation:'Drop, Cover, Hold On under a sturdy table or desk, away from windows and heavy furniture. Doorways and the "triangle of life" are not safer, and moving during the shaking is dangerous.'},{question:"After an earthquake you hear a voice under the rubble of a fallen wall. What helps most?",options:[{text:"From a safe place, note where the voice is and tell the rescue team",correct:!0},{text:"Climb onto the rubble in gloves and a helmet and start digging",correct:!1},{text:"Pull the stones away quickly before the rescue team arrives",correct:!1}],explanation:"Rubble can shift and collapse, even under a person wearing gloves and a helmet. Rescue is the job of trained teams; you help most by reporting where the voice is."}],steps:[{title:"During the shaking: Drop, Cover, Hold On",detail:'Drop to the ground, take cover under a sturdy table and hold on, away from windows and heavy furniture. Do not run outside, do not stand in a doorway and do not trust the "triangle of life".',critical:!0},{title:"Check for injuries",detail:"When the shaking stops, check yourself and the people around you. If someone is trapped or injured, call 115 and 122. Expect aftershocks: if the ground shakes again, Drop, Cover, Hold On again."},{title:"Leave a damaged building",detail:"Use the stairs, never the elevator, and move away from the building, walls and fallen power lines. Do not go back in until specialists have checked it.",critical:!0},{title:"If you smell gas",detail:"Do not touch switches and do not light a flame. Close the gas valve only if it is close and you can reach it safely without going into the area where the gas has collected; otherwise get out, warn others and call 115 from outside.",critical:!0},{title:"Help rescuers from a safe place",detail:"Never climb onto rubble. From a safe place, listen, note where a voice comes from and tell the rescue team. Follow official announcements and the instructions of Civil Defense."}],caseStudy:[{prompt:"You are in a second-floor classroom when strong shaking starts. A classmate shouts that everyone should run for the stairs. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I drop under my sturdy desk, cover my head and hold on, away from the windows, until the shaking stops",correct:!0,feedback:"Drop, Cover, Hold On protects you from falling objects; running while the floor moves is how many people get hurt."},{text:"I run with the others to the stairs to get outside before the building falls",correct:!1,feedback:"Running during the shaking can make you fall or be hit by falling objects; drop, cover and hold on until it stops."},{text:"I stand in the classroom doorway and hold on to the door frame",correct:!1,feedback:"A doorway is not safer and the door can swing and hurt you; take cover under a sturdy desk."},{text:"I go to the window to see what is happening outside",correct:!1,feedback:"Windows can break and cut you; stay away from them and take cover under a sturdy desk."}]},{prompt:"The shaking has stopped. There are cracks in the walls and the teacher is leading the class out. In the corridor near the science laboratory you smell gas; the gas valve is inside the laboratory. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I touch no switch, tell the teacher about the smell and we keep walking out; 115 is called from outside",correct:!0,feedback:"The valve is in the area where the gas has collected, so do not go near it: no sparks, warn others, get out and call 115 from outside."},{text:"I go into the laboratory to close the gas valve, because I know where it is",correct:!1,feedback:"The valve is inside the area where the gas has collected; never go in for a valve. Get out and call 115 from outside."},{text:"I switch on the corridor light so that we can see the way out better",correct:!1,feedback:"A switch can make a spark that ignites the gas; touch no switch and keep walking out."},{text:"We take the elevator, because it is faster than the stairs",correct:!1,feedback:"After an earthquake the elevator can stop and trap you; always use the stairs."}]}]},{id:4,icon:"HeartPulse",accent:"#e11d48",title:"First aid: cardiopulmonary resuscitation (CPR)",subtitle:"Chest compressions, rescue breaths and the AED",summary:"CPR (Cardiopulmonary Resuscitation), made up of chest compressions and rescue breaths, is a life-saving technique used when the heart and breathing stop. Everyone should learn CPR. According to the American Heart Association (AHA), for every minute without CPR and an AED, the chance of survival falls by about 7–10%. The C-A-B order: first chest Compressions, then opening the Airway, then Breathing.",blocks:[{heading:"CPR for adults (30:2)",body:`1. Safety: check your surroundings
2. Responsiveness: shake the person's shoulders and ask loudly, "Are you all right?"
3. Call 122 (ambulance) and ask for an AED; put the phone on speaker
4. Check breathing: look at the chest for 10 seconds or less
5. 30 chest compressions: in the center of the chest, 5–6 cm deep, 100–120 compressions per minute; after each compression, let the chest recoil fully
6. 2 rescue breaths, if you are trained: tilt the head back and lift the chin; blow into the mouth for 1 second per breath, until the chest rises. If you are not trained, or not willing to give breaths: chest compressions only (Hands-Only CPR), without stopping
7. Continue 30:2 (or compressions only) until the ambulance arrives or an AED is brought`,image:"media/images/cpr_steps.png"},{heading:"The AED (defibrillator)",body:`• An AED is an automatic device that checks the heart rhythm and gives an electric shock if one is needed
• Switch the device on and follow its voice instructions
• AED pads: one on the upper right side of the chest (just below the collarbone), the other on the lower left side of the chest (toward the side of the body)
• If the AED says "shock advised": make sure no one is touching the casualty, then press the shock button
• After the shock, immediately resume CPR for 2 minutes
• The AED checks the heart rhythm again every 2 minutes`,image:"media/images/aed_use.png"},{heading:"CPR for children and infants",body:`• Child (from 1 year old until puberty): use one or two hands, about 5 cm deep (one third of the depth of the chest); 30:2 if you are alone, 15:2 if there are two rescuers
• Infant (under 1 year old): two thumbs on the chest with both hands encircling it (two-thumb encircling), or the heel of one hand; about 4 cm deep (one third of the depth of the chest); 30:2 alone, 15:2 with two rescuers. The two-finger method is no longer taught (AHA 2025)
• Breaths for an infant: place your mouth over the infant's mouth and nose together and blow gently
• Children, infants and people who drowned especially need breaths: if you are trained and able, give CPR with breaths; if you cannot, compressions alone are better than nothing
• If you are alone: if you have a phone with you, call 122 immediately on speaker and start CPR; if you have no phone, do 2 minutes of CPR, then go and call 122
• Choking: child: 5 back blows + 5 abdominal thrusts (above the navel, well below the breastbone); infant: 5 back blows (head lower than the chest) + 5 chest thrusts; never give abdominal thrusts to an infant`,image:"media/images/cpr_child.png"}],examples:["30 compressions + 2 breaths = 1 cycle.","Depth: 5–6 cm (adult), about 5 cm (child), about 4 cm (infant).","Rate: 100–120 compressions per minute.","First: call 122 and ask for an AED.","AED: place the pads on the chest and follow the instructions.","Every minute without CPR: the chance of survival falls by about 7–10%.","Infant: two thumbs or the heel of one hand; mouth and nose together.","If there are two people: one gives compressions and the other gets the AED ready."],tips:["Always put your own safety first.","Memorize 30:2.","Push hard and fast: 5–6 cm, 100–120 per minute.","Let the chest recoil fully between compressions.","If an AED is available, use it as soon as possible."],warnings:["Start CPR early! For every minute without CPR, the chance of survival falls by about 7–10%, and after 10 minutes without CPR the chance becomes very low. Do not be afraid of breaking ribs: ribs may break during correct CPR, but life matters more!"],realCase:"In a supermarket in Duhok, a 55-year-old man suddenly collapsed and his heart stopped. A Grade 11 student who had learned CPR immediately started chest compressions while his friend called 122. The student kept up the compressions without stopping until the ambulance arrived and the paramedics took over. Without CPR, the man might have died.",video:"media/videos/lesson_cpr.mp4",game:{instruction:"The CPR steps in the right order: choose the correct ones:",items:[{text:"Safety → responsiveness → 122 → compressions",emoji:"✅",correct:!0},{text:"Compressions → breaths → 122",emoji:"❌",correct:!1},{text:"30 compressions + 2 breaths",emoji:"💓",correct:!0},{text:"10 compressions + 5 breaths",emoji:"❌",correct:!1}]},activity:{prompt:"At school, a student suddenly collapses, does not respond and is not breathing normally. You are the first person there. What will you do?",image:"media/images/activity_cpr.png",options:[{text:"I check that the scene is safe, test whether he responds, call 122 and ask for an AED, check his breathing and start CPR at 30:2 until the ambulance arrives",correct:!0,feedback:"This is the right order: safe scene, response, 122 and an AED, breathing check, then CPR; if not trained, give compressions only."},{text:"I pour water on his face",correct:!1,feedback:"Water on the face does not help someone who is not breathing normally; call 122, ask for an AED and start CPR."},{text:"I let him sleep",correct:!1,feedback:"This is not sleep: no response and no normal breathing mean you call 122, ask for an AED and start CPR."},{text:"I run away",correct:!1,feedback:"Every minute without CPR lowers his chance of survival; stay, call 122, ask for an AED and push hard and fast."}]},quiz:[{question:"What is the ratio of compressions to breaths in adult CPR?",options:[{text:"30 compressions + 2 breaths",correct:!0,feedback:"Adult CPR is 30 compressions, then 2 breaths if you are trained; if not, compressions only without stopping."},{text:"15 compressions + 2 breaths",correct:!1,feedback:"No! 15:2 is for children and infants when there are two rescuers."},{text:"10 compressions + 1 breath",correct:!1,feedback:"That ratio is not taught: adult CPR is 30 compressions and 2 breaths, or compressions only (Hands-Only CPR)."}],explanation:"For adults, trained rescuers give 30 chest compressions and 2 rescue breaths (30:2). If you are not trained or not willing to give breaths, give compressions only, without stopping (Hands-Only CPR)."},{question:"What is the rate of chest compressions in CPR?",options:[{text:"100–120 per minute",correct:!0,feedback:"100–120 per minute is the right rate; push hard and fast and let the chest recoil fully each time."},{text:"60–80 per minute",correct:!1,feedback:"That is too slow!"},{text:"150–180 per minute",correct:!1,feedback:"That is too fast!"}],explanation:"CPR compressions are given at 100–120 per minute: push hard and fast, 5–6 cm deep for adults, and let the chest recoil fully after each one."},{question:"For every minute without CPR, by about how much does the chance of survival fall?",options:[{text:"7–10%",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct! (AHA)"},{text:"1–2%",correct:!1,feedback:"The loss is much faster: about 7–10% for every minute without CPR and an AED (AHA)."},{text:"40–50%",correct:!1,feedback:"The AHA figure is about 7–10% per minute, not 40–50%; still, every minute counts, so start CPR early."}],explanation:"According to the AHA, the chance of survival falls by about 7–10% for every minute without CPR and an AED. That is why you call 122 and start CPR at once."},{question:"An adult collapses and is not breathing normally. You have called 122, but you never learned rescue breaths. What do you do?",options:[{text:"Give chest compressions only, hard and fast, without stopping",correct:!0},{text:"Wait for the ambulance, because CPR without breaths does not help",correct:!1},{text:"Give a few breaths anyway, then wait to see if he wakes up",correct:!1}],explanation:"Untrained or unwilling rescuers give compressions only (Hands-Only CPR): 100–120 per minute, 5–6 cm deep, without stopping until help takes over or an AED is ready."},{question:'The AED says "shock advised". What do you do?',options:[{text:"Make sure nobody touches him, press the shock button and restart CPR at once",correct:!0},{text:"Keep pushing on his chest while the AED gives the shock",correct:!1},{text:"Take the pads off and check his breathing before pressing the button",correct:!1}],explanation:"Nobody may touch the person during the shock. Right after it, restart CPR at once for 2 minutes; the AED checks the heart rhythm again every 2 minutes."}],steps:[{title:"Check that it is safe and test for a response",detail:`Make sure the scene is safe. Tap the person's shoulders and ask loudly, "Are you all right?"`,critical:!0},{title:"Call 122 and ask for an AED",detail:"Call 122 or ask someone to call, put the phone on speaker and follow the dispatcher. Send someone to bring an AED.",critical:!0},{title:"Check breathing for no more than 10 seconds",detail:"Look at the chest. If the person is not breathing normally, start CPR at once.",critical:!0},{title:"Push hard and fast",detail:"Center of the chest, 5–6 cm deep, 100–120 per minute, letting the chest recoil fully. If you are trained and willing: 30 compressions and 2 breaths; if not: compressions only, without stopping (Hands-Only CPR).",critical:!0},{title:"Use the AED as soon as it arrives",detail:"Switch it on and follow its voice. Nobody touches the person during the shock; then restart CPR at once. Continue until the ambulance team takes over.",critical:!0}],caseStudy:[{prompt:"At a football match, a man in the stands suddenly collapses. He does not respond when you tap his shoulders and ask loudly, and he is not breathing normally. Your friend is beside you. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I ask my friend to call 122 on speaker and bring an AED, and I start chest compressions",correct:!0,feedback:"No response and no normal breathing mean CPR now: one person calls 122 and gets the AED while the other pushes hard and fast."},{text:"I put him on his side in the recovery position and wait for the ambulance",correct:!1,feedback:"The recovery position is for someone who is breathing normally; he is not, so start CPR."},{text:"I try to give him some water to drink so that he wakes up",correct:!1,feedback:"Nothing goes into the mouth of a person who does not respond; call 122 and start CPR."},{text:"I wait a few minutes to see whether he starts breathing by himself",correct:!1,feedback:"Every minute without CPR lowers his chance of survival by about 7–10%; start compressions now."}]},{prompt:"You have been pushing for some minutes. Your friend comes back with an AED. You have never learned rescue breaths. What do you do now?",options:[{text:"I keep pushing while my friend turns on the AED and attaches the pads; we stay clear for the shock, then I restart at once",correct:!0,feedback:"Keeping compressions going until the AED analyzes, staying clear for the shock and restarting at once give him the best chance."},{text:"I stop CPR because he looks pale and I am afraid of breaking his ribs",correct:!1,feedback:"Ribs may break during correct CPR, but life matters more; keep going and use the AED."},{text:"I keep my hands on his chest during the shock so that I do not lose time",correct:!1,feedback:"Anyone touching him during the shock can be hurt and the shock may not work; stay clear, then restart at once."},{text:"I stop compressions and try breaths, though I have never learned how",correct:!1,feedback:"If you are not trained, give compressions only, without stopping (Hands-Only CPR); the AED and compressions matter most."}]}]},{id:5,icon:"Biohazard",accent:"#9333ea",title:"Chemical and hazardous materials (HazMat)",subtitle:"Identification and handling",summary:"HazMat (hazardous materials) are dangerous substances: chemical, biological, radiological and nuclear (CBRN), as well as explosive materials. Learning to recognize them and to handle them correctly is important for middle and high school students.",blocks:[{heading:"GHS and hazardous material symbols",body:`GHS (Globally Harmonized System): the worldwide system for classifying and labeling chemicals. It has 9 pictograms:
• Skull and crossbones = toxic and deadly
• Flame = flammable
• Flame over circle = oxidizer
• Exploding bomb = explosive
• Drops on a hand and on metal = corrosive (Corrosive)
• Gas cylinder = gas under pressure
• Exclamation mark (!) = irritant and minor harm
• Human body with a star on the chest = serious health hazard (such as cancer)
• Fish and tree = hazard to the environment

GHS has no pictogram for radioactive materials; that symbol is found in the UN regulations for the transport of dangerous goods (Class 7).

The NFPA 704 diamond (4 colors):
• Red = flammability (0–4)
• Blue = health (0–4)
• Yellow = instability (0–4)
• White = special hazards: OX (oxidizer), W with a line through it (do not use water), SA (simple asphyxiant gas)`,image:"media/images/ghs_symbols.png"},{heading:"SDS and personal protective equipment (PPE)",body:`SDS (Safety Data Sheet): the safety information sheet, which contains 16 sections of information about each chemical:
• Section 2: hazard identification
• Section 4: first aid
• Section 5: firefighting measures
• Section 8: personal protective equipment (PPE)

PPE levels:
• A: fully encapsulating chemical suit + self-contained breathing apparatus (SCBA)
• B: chemical-protective clothing + SCBA
• C: chemical-protective clothing + filter mask
• D: ordinary work clothes + gloves and goggles`,image:"media/images/sds_ppe.png"},{heading:"Decontamination and procedure",body:`If a hazardous material is released:
1. Raise the alarm: call 115 (and 122 as well if there are casualties)
2. Get out: move to the side the wind is coming from (upwind), so that the wind does not carry gas and dust toward you
3. Move as far away as the authorities tell you, and follow their instructions
4. Do not touch it and do not smell it
5. Decontamination (remove, remove, remove): move away into fresh air, take off your outer clothing and blot or brush the substance off the skin with a dry cloth or tissue; if the skin itches or burns, rinse it with plenty of water. If it gets into your eyes, rinse them with clean water
6. Wait for the Civil Defense HazMat team`,image:"media/images/decontamination.png"}],examples:["Skull = toxic.","Flame = flammable.","Drops on a hand = corrosive.","Exploding bomb = explosive.","SDS: 16 sections.","PPE levels: A, B, C, D.","Move to the side the wind is coming from (upwind).","Move as far away as the authorities tell you."],tips:["Learn the 9 GHS pictograms.","Read the SDS before using any substance.","Use the appropriate PPE.","Move to the side the wind is coming from."],warnings:["Never touch, smell or taste an unknown chemical! Always move to the side the wind is coming from (upwind) and stay as far away as the authorities tell you. Call 115 so that the HazMat team comes!"],realCase:"At a factory in Sulaymaniyah, a quantity of acid spilled. A trained worker immediately raised the alarm, moved all the workers to the side the wind was coming from and called 115 so that the HazMat team would come. His training had taught him exactly what to do.",video:"media/videos/lesson_hazmat.mp4",game:{instruction:"Match each symbol with its meaning; choose the correct ones:",items:[{text:"Skull and crossbones = toxic",emoji:"☠️",correct:!0},{text:"Flame = a harmless substance",emoji:"🔥",correct:!1},{text:"Corrosive symbol = harmless to the skin",emoji:"⚗️",correct:!1},{text:"Exclamation mark (!) = health hazard (irritant)",emoji:"⚠️",correct:!0}]},activity:{prompt:"In the laboratory, a bottle of acid has broken and spilled. What will you do?",image:"media/images/activity_hazmat.png",options:[{text:"I warn everyone, we leave the laboratory toward the side the wind is coming from, I alert the teacher and we call 115, and I do not touch it",correct:!0,feedback:"Warning everyone, leaving upwind, telling the teacher, calling 115 and not touching the acid follows the lesson's procedure exactly."},{text:"I clean it up with a cloth",correct:!1,feedback:"Do not touch a spilled chemical, even with a cloth; leave upwind, warn others, call 115 and wait for the HazMat team."},{text:"I pour water on it",correct:!1,feedback:"Do not treat the spill yourself: do not touch it, get out upwind, call 115 and wait for the HazMat team."},{text:"I do nothing",correct:!1,feedback:"Staying near a spill puts everyone at risk: warn others, leave upwind, tell the teacher and call 115."}]},quiz:[{question:"In the NFPA diamond, what does the color red mean?",options:[{text:"Flammability",correct:!0,feedback:"Red shows flammability, rated from 0 to 4; the higher the number, the more easily the material catches fire."},{text:"Health hazard",correct:!1,feedback:"That is blue!"},{text:"Instability",correct:!1,feedback:"That is yellow!"}],explanation:"In the NFPA 704 diamond, red = flammability, blue = health, yellow = instability (each rated 0–4) and white = special hazards such as OX, W or SA."},{question:"Why do we move to the side the wind is coming from (upwind)?",options:[{text:"So that the wind does not carry gas and dust toward us",correct:!0,feedback:"Upwind, the wind blows the gas and dust away from you instead of carrying them toward you."},{text:"So that we reach home or the assembly point sooner",correct:!1,feedback:"The direction is chosen for safety, not convenience: upwind keeps the gas and dust from blowing toward you."},{text:"It makes no difference which way the wind is blowing from",correct:!1,feedback:"It matters a lot: if the wind blows toward you, it carries the gas and dust to you."}],explanation:"Moving to the side the wind is coming from (upwind) means the wind carries gas and dust away from you, not toward you. Then stay as far away as the authorities tell you."},{question:"A chemical splashes onto your arm and clothes. What should you do?",options:[{text:"Move into fresh air, take off the outer clothing and blot the substance off with a dry cloth",correct:!0},{text:"Keep the clothes on so that they protect the skin, and wait without moving for the HazMat team",correct:!1},{text:"Wipe the liquid off with your bare hand and carry on, because a small amount cannot cause harm",correct:!1}],explanation:"Remove yourself, remove outer clothing, remove the substance: blot or brush it off the skin with a dry cloth. If the skin itches or burns, rinse it with plenty of water; if it gets into your eyes, rinse them with clean water."},{question:"A tanker is leaking gas, and the wind is blowing from the north toward the south. Where do you go?",options:[{text:"North of the leak, toward the side the wind comes from",correct:!0},{text:"South of the leak, so that the wind helps you move away",correct:!1},{text:"Into a low ditch beside the tanker, to hide from the gas",correct:!1}],explanation:"Upwind, the wind carries gas and dust away from you; downwind, it carries them toward you, and a ditch beside the tanker keeps you close to the danger. Stay as far away as the authorities tell you."},{question:"At a chemical spill, which action is wrong?",options:[{text:"Smelling the liquid to find out what it is",correct:!0},{text:"Telling the teacher and calling 115",correct:!1},{text:"Moving upwind and keeping others away",correct:!1},{text:"Telling the HazMat team what the label showed",correct:!1}],explanation:"Never touch, smell or taste an unknown chemical. Raising the alarm, moving upwind, keeping others away and passing on what the label showed are all right."}],steps:[{title:"Keep away and do not touch",detail:"Never touch, smell or taste an unknown chemical, and do not try to clean up a spill yourself.",critical:!0},{title:"Warn others and raise the alarm",detail:"Warn everyone nearby, tell a teacher or another adult and call 115; call 122 as well if someone is hurt.",critical:!0},{title:"Move upwind",detail:"Go to the side the wind is coming from (upwind), so that the wind carries gas and dust away from you, and stay as far away as the authorities tell you.",critical:!0},{title:"Remove, remove, remove",detail:"If the substance is on you: move into fresh air, take off your outer clothing and blot or brush the substance off your skin with a dry cloth. If the skin itches or burns, rinse it with plenty of water; rinse the eyes with clean water."},{title:"Wait for the HazMat team",detail:"Stay at the safe place, follow their instructions and tell them what spilled if you know, for example the name or pictogram on the label."}],caseStudy:[{prompt:"Walking home from school, you see a tanker truck lying on its side. A liquid is leaking onto the road and there is a sharp smell. Some people are walking closer to film it. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I stay away, tell the people filming to move back, go with them to the side the wind is coming from and call 115",correct:!0,feedback:"Distance, warning others and moving upwind protect everyone; calling 115 brings the HazMat team."},{text:"I go closer to read the sign on the tank so that I can tell 115 what it is",correct:!1,feedback:"Going closer to read a sign puts you in the vapor; report from a safe distance what you can see."},{text:"I stay where I am and film too, because I am already far enough away",correct:!1,feedback:"You cannot judge a safe distance from a smell; move upwind and as far away as the authorities tell you."},{text:"I walk through the leaking liquid to help the driver out of the cab",correct:!1,feedback:"Walking through the spill can contaminate and hurt you; call 115 and 122 and let the HazMat team reach the driver."}]},{prompt:"You are now upwind with the others. A boy who had been close says his sleeve and arm were splashed, and his skin is starting to burn. What do you do?",options:[{text:"We call 122 too; he takes off the splashed outer clothing, blots the substance off with a dry cloth and rinses the skin with plenty of water",correct:!0,feedback:"Remove, remove, remove: away from the source, outer clothing off, substance off; burning skin is rinsed with plenty of water."},{text:"He wipes the liquid off with his bare hand and keeps the shirt on until he gets home",correct:!1,feedback:"Bare hands spread the chemical, and contaminated clothing keeps it on the skin; take the outer clothing off and blot the substance away."},{text:"He washes his arm in the puddle at the side of the road near the truck",correct:!1,feedback:"The puddle may be the leaking chemical itself and is next to the danger; use clean water, away from the truck."},{text:"We do nothing, because the burning will stop by itself after a while",correct:!1,feedback:"A burning feeling means the chemical is still acting; remove it now and call 122."}]}]},{id:6,icon:"Waves",accent:"#0891b2",title:"Floods and disasters: planning",subtitle:"Warning and preparedness",summary:"Floods and natural disasters require advance planning. At the high school level, we learn about warning systems, the family emergency plan and the role of students in safety teams.",blocks:[{heading:"Warning systems",body:`• EWS (Early Warning System): a system that gives early warning before a disaster
• Follow official warnings and announcements, and do what they tell you
• Warning levels and channels differ from country to country: learn the system that the authorities in your area use
• Flood gauge: shows the height of the water
• Weather warnings: through radio, television and cell phones`,image:"media/images/ews_system.png"},{heading:"Family emergency plan",body:`• Assembly points: 2 points (one nearby and one farther away)
• Exit routes: 2 routes out of the home
• Communication: the phone numbers of all family members
• Emergency kit: for 72 hours (water: about 4 liters per person per day)
• Documents: copies kept in a safe place away from water
• Practice: test the plan with your family at least twice a year`,image:"media/images/family_plan.png"},{heading:"The student's role in safety",body:`• CERT (Community Emergency Response Team): community volunteer teams that help during emergencies
• Trained students can become first helpers
• Roles: raising the alarm, first aid, helping affected people
• Coordination with 115 and the relevant authorities
• Recognizing the hazards in the surrounding area`,image:"media/images/cert_team.png"}],examples:["Follow official warnings!","Learn the warning system used in your area.","72-hour emergency kit.","The phone numbers of all family members.","2 assembly points.","2 exit routes.","CERT: community help teams.","A radio to listen for warnings."],tips:["Make a family plan.","Keep your emergency kit ready.","Practice the plan at least twice a year."],warnings:["Floods are among the most dangerous natural disasters! Just 15 cm of moving flood water can knock a person down, and 30 cm can carry a car away. Never walk or drive into flood water!"],realCase:"A family in Zakho had an emergency plan. When the flood came, they got out quickly and gathered at the assembly point. Families without a plan lose valuable time.",video:"media/videos/lesson_flood_adv.mp4",game:{instruction:"How dangerous is moving flood water? Choose the correct ones:",items:[{text:"15 cm can knock a person down",emoji:"🚶",correct:!0},{text:"Shallow water is not dangerous at all",emoji:"🤷",correct:!1},{text:"30 cm can carry a car away",emoji:"🚗",correct:!0},{text:"You can drive a car through the water",emoji:"✅",correct:!1}]},activity:{prompt:"An official flood warning tells people in the area around your home to leave. There are 2 hours left before the water reaches it. What will you do?",image:"media/images/activity_flood_adv.png",options:[{text:"I start the family plan, take the emergency kit, alert the family members and we go to the assembly point on high ground",correct:!0,feedback:"The official warning tells you to leave: starting the family plan, taking the kit, alerting everyone and reaching high ground saves time."},{text:"I just wait",correct:!1,feedback:"The warning tells you to leave; waiting wastes the time you have. Start the family plan and go to the assembly point."},{text:"I stay at home",correct:!1,feedback:"The warning tells you to leave; staying risks being trapped by flood water. Just 15 cm of moving water can knock a person down."},{text:"I go to the supermarket",correct:!1,feedback:"Your emergency kit should already be ready; when the warning tells you to leave, go with your family to the assembly point, not shopping."}]},quiz:[{question:"What does EWS mean?",options:[{text:"An early warning system before a disaster",correct:!0,feedback:"EWS gives early warning before a disaster, so people have time to act; follow the official warnings."},{text:"A piece of safety equipment kept in every home",correct:!1,feedback:"EWS is a warning system, not a single device: it warns people early, before a disaster."},{text:"A type of fire extinguisher used in schools",correct:!1,feedback:"EWS has nothing to do with extinguishers; it is the Early Warning System that warns before a disaster."}],explanation:"EWS (Early Warning System) warns people before a disaster, so families have time to follow their plan. Warning levels and channels differ from country to country: learn the system the authorities in your area use."},{question:"A flood warning is announced for your area. What should you do?",options:[{text:"Follow the official warnings and announcements and do what they tell you",correct:!0},{text:"Wait until you can see the water rising near your home before doing anything",correct:!1},{text:"Do whatever your neighbors decide, whatever the official announcements say",correct:!1}],explanation:"Official warnings give your family time to follow its plan. Warning levels and channels differ from country to country, so learn the system the authorities in your area use and do what it tells you."},{question:"What does the lesson teach about moving flood water?",options:[{text:"Just 15 cm of it can knock a person down, and 30 cm can carry a car away",correct:!0},{text:"It is only dangerous once it is deeper than an adult's waist, so wading is safe",correct:!1},{text:"A car is heavy enough to stay on the road in it, as long as you drive slowly",correct:!1}],explanation:"Moving flood water is much stronger than it looks: just 15 cm can knock a person down and 30 cm can carry a car away. Never walk or drive into flood water."},{question:"Why does a family plan have two assembly points?",options:[{text:"If one cannot be reached, everyone knows where to go instead",correct:!0},{text:"So that the family can split into two equal groups",correct:!1},{text:"So that one is used in summer and the other in winter",correct:!1}],explanation:"A flood, a fire or a blocked road can make one place impossible to reach. With a second assembly point agreed in advance, the family can still find each other."},{question:"Your family has four people. About how much water do you keep for 3 days?",options:[{text:"About 48 liters",correct:!0},{text:"About 12 liters",correct:!1},{text:"About 16 liters",correct:!1},{text:"About 4 liters",correct:!1}],explanation:"The plan counts about 4 liters per person per day: 4 liters × 4 people × 3 days = about 48 liters."}],steps:[{title:"Make the family plan in advance",detail:"Agree on 2 assembly points (one nearby and one farther away) and 2 routes out of the home, write down every family member's phone number and keep copies of documents in a safe place away from water."},{title:"Keep the emergency kit ready",detail:"A kit for 72 hours: about 4 liters of water per person per day, food, medicine, a flashlight and a battery-powered radio to listen for warnings."},{title:"Follow official warnings at once",detail:"When an official warning tells you to leave, start the family plan, take the kit and go to the assembly point on high ground. Do not wait until you see the water.",critical:!0},{title:"Never walk or drive into flood water",detail:"Just 15 cm of moving water can knock a person down, and 30 cm can carry a car away. Turn back and use another route.",critical:!0},{title:"Practice the plan",detail:"Test the plan with your family at least twice a year, so that everyone knows where to go."}],caseStudy:[{prompt:"After two days of heavy rain, an official announcement on the radio tells people in your neighborhood to get ready to leave. Your parents are at work and you are at home with your younger sister. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I call my parents, get the kit and the documents ready and follow our family plan and the official instructions",correct:!0,feedback:"The announcement gives you time; calling your parents, getting the kit ready and following the plan use that time well."},{text:"I wait to see whether the water really reaches our street before doing anything",correct:!1,feedback:"Waiting for the water wastes the time the warning gives you; start the family plan now."},{text:"I ask on social media whether the warning is true and wait for answers",correct:!1,feedback:"Follow official announcements, not rumors; waiting for answers online wastes time."},{text:"I go to the river with friends to see how high the water already is",correct:!1,feedback:"Going near a rising river is dangerous; stay with your sister and follow the plan."}]},{prompt:"Your parents tell you to walk with your sister to the assembly point on high ground. On the way, water is flowing across the road. It looks shallow. What do you do?",options:[{text:"We do not cross; we turn back, take the second route to higher ground and tell our parents",correct:!0,feedback:"Moving water is stronger than it looks; 15 cm can knock a person down. The second route in the plan is for exactly this."},{text:"We hold hands tightly and wade across quickly, because it looks shallow",correct:!1,feedback:"Holding hands does not stop the current; 15 cm of moving water can knock you both down."},{text:"We ask a passing driver to take us across the water in his car",correct:!1,feedback:"30 cm of moving water can carry a car away; never drive into flood water."},{text:"We wait in the middle of the road until the water goes down",correct:!1,feedback:"The water may rise, not fall; turn back and use another route to high ground."}]}]},{id:7,icon:"Zap",accent:"#ca8a04",title:"Electricity: high voltage",subtitle:"Hazards and safe rescue",summary:"High-voltage electricity can kill a person and cause deep burns. Learning to recognize the hazards and the methods of safe rescue is very important.",blocks:[{heading:"The danger of high voltage",body:`• Household voltage: 220 volts; even this can kill!
• High voltage: from 11 to 400 kilovolts (kV) in power transmission lines
• It is the current (amperes) passing through the body that kills a person; high voltage drives a larger current through the body. Just 0.1 amperes (100 milliamperes) through the heart can kill
• High-voltage electricity can jump through the air (arcing) without you touching it; therefore stay at least 10 meters away
• Electrical burns are deep and may not be visible from the outside`,image:"media/images/high_voltage.png"},{heading:"Rescuing electric shock casualties",body:`1. Do not touch the casualty until the electricity has been cut off!
2. If it is high voltage: stay at least 10 meters away and call 115; only specialist teams can cut off the electricity
3. Household electricity: switch it off at the fuse, the circuit breaker or the main switch
4. If you cannot cut off the household electricity: stand on something dry and use a non-conducting object (dry wood, plastic) to move the casualty away from the source of electricity. Never do this with high voltage!
5. Call 122; if the casualty is not breathing, start CPR
6. Check for burns (where the electricity entered the body and where it left) and, after the electricity has been cut off, cool them with cool running water for 20 minutes`,image:"media/images/electric_rescue.png"},{heading:"Protecting yourself",body:`• Do not go near transformers or overhead power lines
• A power line that has fallen to the ground = danger! Stay at least 10 meters away
• If you are in a car and a line falls on it: stay inside and call 115. If the car catches fire, jump clear with both feet together, without touching the car and the ground at the same time
• If you are near a fallen line: move away with very small steps and your feet together (shuffle), without lifting your feet off the ground
• During lightning and rain: stay inside a building`,image:"media/images/electric_prevention.png"}],examples:["0.1 amperes through the heart can kill.","High voltage: stay at least 10 meters away.","First, cut off the electricity.","Use a non-conducting object (household electricity only).","If a line falls on your car, stay inside.","Small steps (shuffle step): without lifting your feet off the ground.","CPR if needed.","Call 122; for fallen lines, 115."],tips:["First, cut off the electricity.","Do not touch the casualty until the electricity has been cut off.","Use a non-conducting object.","Call 122 for the casualty and 115 for a fallen line."],warnings:["Never touch an electric shock casualty until the electricity has been cut off! The electricity will pass through your body too, and you will both become victims. Stay at least 10 meters away from high voltage!"],realCase:"In a village in Duhok, after a strong wind, a high-voltage power line fell to the ground. A trained 16-year-old knew that he must not go near it. He warned everyone to keep away and called 115. The repair teams came and removed the danger.",video:"media/videos/lesson_highvolt.mp4",game:{instruction:"When someone is getting an electric shock, which first actions are correct?",items:[{text:"Cut off the electricity",emoji:"🔌",correct:!0},{text:"Touch the casualty",emoji:"🤚",correct:!1},{text:"Call 122",emoji:"📞",correct:!0},{text:"Pull them away from the electricity with a piece of iron",emoji:"🔧",correct:!1}]},activity:{prompt:"In the laboratory, your friend got an electric shock and collapsed. The wire is still touching his body. What will you do?",image:"media/images/activity_electric.png",options:[{text:"First I switch off the fuse or the circuit breaker, then, if needed, I move the wire away from him with a non-conducting object without touching his body, and I call 122; if he is not breathing, I start CPR",correct:!0,feedback:"Cutting the power first keeps you from becoming a second victim; then call 122 and start CPR if he is not breathing."},{text:"I pull him away with my hands",correct:!1,feedback:"The current would pass through you too and you would both become victims; cut off the electricity first."},{text:"I pour water on him",correct:!1,feedback:"Water conducts electricity and does not cut the current; switch off the fuse or breaker first and do not touch him."},{text:"I do nothing",correct:!1,feedback:"He needs help fast: cut off the electricity at the fuse or breaker, call 122 and start CPR if needed."}]},quiz:[{question:"How much current (amperes) through the heart can kill a person?",options:[{text:"0.1 amperes",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct! Only 100 milliamperes."},{text:"100 amperes",correct:!1,feedback:"Much less than this can kill!"},{text:"1000 amperes",correct:!1,feedback:"Far less is enough: just 0.1 amperes (100 milliamperes) through the heart can kill."}],explanation:"It is the current through the body that kills: just 0.1 amperes (100 milliamperes) through the heart can be deadly, and even 220-volt household electricity can kill."},{question:"A person is lying next to a fallen high-voltage power line. What should you do?",options:[{text:"Stay at least 10 meters away and call 115; only specialist teams can cut off the electricity",correct:!0},{text:"Stand on something dry and quickly push the person away from the line with a long, dry, non-conducting wooden stick",correct:!1},{text:"Pull the person away by the clothes as fast as you can, taking care not to touch the bare skin",correct:!1}],explanation:"High-voltage electricity can jump through the air, so stay at least 10 meters away and call 115. A non-conducting object is only for household electricity; never use it with high voltage."},{question:"A power line falls on the car you are in, and the car is not on fire. What should you do?",options:[{text:"Stay inside the car and call 115 for help",correct:!0},{text:"Get out at once and run away from the car fast",correct:!1},{text:"Climb out through a window and walk away",correct:!1}],explanation:"The line may be live, and inside the car you are safer: stay inside and call 115. Only if the car catches fire, jump clear with both feet together, without touching the car and the ground at the same time."},{question:"At home, a friend is stuck to a kettle cable that is giving shocks. What do you do first?",options:[{text:"Switch off the power at the fuse, breaker or main switch",correct:!0},{text:"Pull your friend away quickly by the arm",correct:!1},{text:"Throw water on the cable to stop the current",correct:!1},{text:"Grab the cable and pull the plug out of the socket",correct:!1}],explanation:"Never touch a person who is still in contact with electricity. Cut the power first at the fuse, the circuit breaker or the main switch, then call 122 and start CPR if needed."},{question:"The power is off. The casualty has a burn where the current entered the hand. What do you do?",options:[{text:"Call 122 and cool the burn under cool running water for 20 minutes",correct:!0},{text:"Put ice straight on the burn so that it stops hurting",correct:!1},{text:"Leave it alone, because a small electrical burn is never deep",correct:!1},{text:"Spread butter or toothpaste on the burn and cover it",correct:!1}],explanation:"Electrical burns can be deep even when they look small. After the power is off, cool them under cool running water for 20 minutes, never with ice, butter or toothpaste, and call 122."}],steps:[{title:"Do not touch the casualty",detail:"Never touch a person who is still in contact with electricity; the current will pass through you too.",critical:!0},{title:"High voltage: keep 10 meters away",detail:"With a fallen or high-voltage line, stay at least 10 meters away, keep others back and call 115. Only specialist teams can cut off the power.",critical:!0},{title:"Household power: switch it off",detail:"Switch off the electricity at the fuse, the circuit breaker or the main switch. If you cannot, stand on something dry and move the source away with a dry, non-conducting object such as wood or plastic; never with high voltage.",critical:!0},{title:"Call 122 and check breathing",detail:"When the power is off, call 122. If the person is not breathing normally, start CPR.",critical:!0},{title:"Cool the burns",detail:"Electrical burns can be deep even if they look small. After the power is off, cool them with cool running water for 20 minutes; no ice, butter or toothpaste."}],caseStudy:[{prompt:"After a storm, you and a friend see a power line lying across a path in a field. A dog lies still right next to it, and your friend wants to go and check on the dog. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I stop my friend, we stay at least 10 meters away, keep others back and call 115",correct:!0,feedback:"A fallen line may be live and electricity can jump through the air; distance and 115 are the only safe help, even for the dog."},{text:"I push the line away from the dog with a long, dry stick",correct:!1,feedback:"A dry stick is only for household electricity, never for a fallen or high-voltage line. Stay 10 meters away and call 115."},{text:"We walk around to reach the dog from the other side, away from the line",correct:!1,feedback:"Getting close to the dog means getting close to the line; stay at least 10 meters away and call 115."},{text:"I touch the line quickly with my shoe to see whether it is still live",correct:!1,feedback:"You cannot tell whether a line is live by touching it, and it can kill you. Stay away and call 115."}]},{prompt:"While you wait for 115, you realize that you are standing much closer to the line than 10 meters. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I move away with very small shuffling steps, feet together, without lifting them off the ground",correct:!0,feedback:"This is how the lesson teaches you to move away from a fallen line; keep going until you are at least 10 meters away."},{text:"I run away with long jumps to get clear as fast as I can",correct:!1,feedback:"Long jumps and running are not how to leave a fallen line; shuffle away with small steps, feet together."},{text:"I stay exactly where I am until the repair team arrives",correct:!1,feedback:"You are too close; move away carefully with small shuffling steps until you are at least 10 meters away."},{text:"I lie flat on the ground so that I am lower than the line",correct:!1,feedback:"Lying down keeps you close to the line; shuffle away with small steps, feet together."}]}]},{id:8,icon:"Fuel",accent:"#059669",title:"Gas leaks and explosions",subtitle:"Natural gas and bottled gas (LPG)",summary:"Natural gas and bottled gas (LPG) are both flammable and explosive. This lesson explains the physical properties of gas and advanced self-protection procedures.",blocks:[{heading:"Natural gas versus LPG",body:`• Natural gas (mostly methane): lighter than air and rises upward (supplied through pipes)
• LPG (propane and butane): heavier than air and collects low down, near the floor (supplied in cylinders)
• LEL (lower explosive limit): the lowest concentration of gas in air that can catch fire or explode. UEL (upper explosive limit): above this concentration, the mixture does not catch fire
• Natural gas (methane): LEL about 5%, UEL about 15%
• LPG (propane): LEL about 2.1%, UEL about 9.5%; so a smaller concentration of LPG can explode
• Mercaptan: a distinctive smell added so that gas can be detected, because the gas itself has no smell`,image:"media/images/gas_types.png"},{heading:"Detection and procedure",body:`• Smell: a smell of rotten eggs (mercaptan)
• Sound: the hissing sound of gas from a pipe or cylinder
• Other signs: dried-out grass and trees near an underground pipe, or frost and cold at the point where LPG is leaking

Procedure:
1. Do not light any flame (matches, lighter)! Do not switch electrical switches on or off, and do not start a generator or a car nearby!
2. Do not use a phone inside
3. Open the windows and doors on your way out, if you can
4. Close the gas valve only if it is close and you can reach it safely, without going into the area where the gas has collected; otherwise do not go near it
5. Get out and warn others
6. Call 115 from outside the building and notify the gas supplier`,image:"media/images/gas_procedure.png"},{heading:"Explosions and self-protection",body:`• BLEVE (Boiling Liquid Expanding Vapor Explosion): a large explosion that happens when an LPG cylinder is heated in a fire and bursts
• Fire + gas = explosion, if the gas concentration is between the LEL and the UEL

Self-protection:
• Install a gas detector at home
• Test pipes with soapy water (bubble test); never test them with a flame
• Do not use gas heaters or gas appliances in a bedroom
• Close the gas valve when you are not using it`,image:"media/images/gas_prevention.png"}],examples:["Natural gas rises / LPG collects low down.","Smell of rotten eggs = gas.","Do not strike matches or lighters!","Open the windows.","Close the gas valve only if it is safe to reach.","Call 115 from outside.","Install a gas detector.","Soapy water test (bubble test) for pipes."],tips:["Install a gas detector.","Test the pipes regularly.","Do not use gas heaters in a bedroom.","Always close the gas valve after use."],warnings:["Do not use a phone in a room full of gas! A phone can also produce a spark and cause an explosion. Call 115 from outside the building."],realCase:"In an apartment in Duhok, gas had leaked and no one had noticed. When someone switched on a light, the spark from the switch caused an explosion. If there had been a gas detector, it would have given a warning before the explosion.",video:"media/videos/lesson_gas_adv.mp4",game:{instruction:"Which way does LPG (cylinder gas) move?",items:[{text:"Downward (it is heavier than air)",emoji:"⬇️",correct:!0},{text:"Upward",emoji:"⬆️",correct:!1},{text:"It spreads evenly everywhere",emoji:"💨",correct:!1},{text:"It has no smell and cannot be detected",emoji:"👃",correct:!1}]},activity:{prompt:"You arrive home and smell gas as soon as you open the door. The light inside is on and your phone is in your hand. What will you do?",image:"media/images/activity_gas_adv.png",options:[{text:"I do not go in and I do not touch the light switch; I move away from the house, warn my family and the neighbors and call 115 once I am well away from the house",correct:!0,feedback:"A switch or phone can spark in gas: stay out, warn others and call 115 once you are well away."},{text:"I touch the light switch",correct:!1,feedback:"A light switch can spark and ignite the gas, whether you switch it on or off; leave it and get out."},{text:"I use my phone inside the house",correct:!1,feedback:"A phone can also make a spark and cause an explosion; call 115 only once you are outside and away."},{text:"I stay there",correct:!1,feedback:"A single spark can make the gas explode; move away, warn your family and neighbors, then call 115 from outside."}]},quiz:[{question:"What does LEL mean?",options:[{text:"The lowest gas level in air that can explode (about 5% for natural gas)",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct! For LPG (propane) it is about 2.1%."},{text:"A flammable gas used for cooking and heating, like LPG or natural gas",correct:!1,feedback:"LEL is not a gas: it is the lowest concentration of gas in air that can catch fire or explode."},{text:"A type of explosion that happens when a gas cylinder overheats in the sun",correct:!1,feedback:"No! LEL is a limit, not an explosion."}],explanation:"LEL (lower explosive limit) is the lowest concentration of gas in air that can catch fire or explode: about 5% for natural gas and about 2.1% for LPG."},{question:"You smell gas at home, and the gas valve is in the kitchen where the gas has collected. What should you do?",options:[{text:"Do not go near the valve; get out, warn others and call 115 from outside",correct:!0},{text:"Hold your breath, run into the kitchen and close the valve, then open all the windows and leave",correct:!1},{text:"Switch on the kitchen light first so that you can find the valve quickly and close it",correct:!1}],explanation:"Close the valve only if it is close and you can reach it safely, without going into the area where the gas has collected. Otherwise do not go near it: get out, warn others and call 115 from outside the building."},{question:"Why must you not use a phone in a room full of gas?",options:[{text:"It can make a spark that sets off an explosion",correct:!0},{text:"Its signal makes the gas spread faster through the room",correct:!1},{text:"The gas can damage the phone's battery and its screen",correct:!1}],explanation:"A phone, like a light switch, can make a spark, and one spark can make the gas explode. Get out first, then call 115 from outside the building."},{question:"LPG leaks in a basement storeroom. Why is this especially dangerous?",options:[{text:"LPG is heavier than air and collects low down, near the floor",correct:!0},{text:"LPG rises quickly and escapes through the ceiling on its own",correct:!1},{text:"LPG becomes harmless as soon as it touches a cold floor",correct:!1}],explanation:"LPG (propane and butane) is heavier than air, so in a low room such as a basement it collects near the floor. A smaller concentration of LPG than of natural gas can explode."},{question:"How do you check a gas pipe joint for a leak?",options:[{text:"Brush soapy water on the joint and look for bubbles",correct:!0},{text:"Move a lighter flame slowly along the pipe and joint",correct:!1},{text:"Turn the cooker on and off several times and listen",correct:!1}],explanation:"Test pipes with soapy water (the bubble test): bubbles show a leak. Never test with a flame. If you smell gas, follow the leak procedure and call 115 from outside."}],steps:[{title:"Recognize the signs",detail:"A smell of rotten eggs (mercaptan) or the hissing sound of gas from a pipe or cylinder means a leak."},{title:"Make no spark",detail:"Do not light any flame, do not switch lights or appliances on or off, do not use a phone inside and do not start a generator or a car nearby.",critical:!0},{title:"The valve only if it is close and safe",detail:"Close the gas valve only if it is close and you can reach it without going into the area where the gas has collected. Otherwise do not go near it.",critical:!0},{title:"Get out and warn others",detail:"Open windows and doors on your way out if you can, get everyone out and warn the neighbors.",critical:!0},{title:"Call 115 from outside",detail:"Call 115 and the gas supplier once you are well away from the building. Do not go back in until Civil Defense says it is safe."}],caseStudy:[{prompt:"In the evening you walk into the kitchen and smell rotten eggs. You hear a hiss from behind the cooker, where the gas cylinder and its valve are, and the smell is strongest there. The kitchen light is off. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I leave the light off, do not go behind the cooker, leave the kitchen and warn my family to get out",correct:!0,feedback:"The valve is where the gas has collected, so do not go near it; no switches, and get everyone out."},{text:"I switch on the light so that I can see where the gas is leaking from",correct:!1,feedback:"A light switch can make a spark that ignites the gas, whether you switch it on or off."},{text:"I hold my breath and reach behind the cooker to close the cylinder valve",correct:!1,feedback:"Holding your breath does not stop a spark or an explosion; never go into the gas area for a valve."},{text:"I use my phone's flashlight to look for the leak behind the cooker",correct:!1,feedback:"A phone can make a spark too; use it only once you are outside and well away."}]},{prompt:"Your family is outside. Your uncle wants to go back in to open the windows and carry the cylinder out, and a neighbor nearby is lighting a cigarette. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I ask my uncle not to go back in, ask the neighbor to put out the cigarette and call 115 from well away from the house",correct:!0,feedback:"Nobody goes back into the gas, there are no flames nearby, and the call is made from a safe distance."},{text:"I help my uncle carry the cylinder out, because two people are faster",correct:!1,feedback:"Going back into the gas puts you both in danger of an explosion; wait for 115."},{text:"I call 115 from just inside the front door, where the phone signal is better",correct:!1,feedback:"A phone can spark inside the gas area; call from outside and well away."},{text:"We start the car parked by the house to drive away quickly",correct:!1,feedback:"Starting a car nearby can ignite the gas; walk away and call 115."}]}]},{id:9,icon:"DoorOpen",accent:"#7c3aed",title:"Safe evacuation planning",subtitle:"ICS and emergency management",summary:"ICS (Incident Command System) is an international system for managing emergencies. At the high school level, students learn the ICS roles and advanced evacuation planning.",blocks:[{heading:"ICS: the command system",body:`ICS works through 5 main sections:
1. Command: the decision-maker; one person oversees all activities
2. Operations: the working teams (firefighting, rescue, first aid)
3. Planning: gathering information and drawing up the plan
4. Logistics: supplies, transport, food, facilities
5. Finance and Administration (Finance/Admin): record-keeping and expenses

At school:
• The principal or senior teacher = commander
• Teachers = operations teams
• Trained students = helpers`,image:"media/images/ics_structure.png"},{heading:"Advanced evacuation plan",body:`• Know the building: all doors, stairs and emergency exits
• Know the hazards: gas, electricity, chemicals
• Main route + alternative route
• Assembly point: main + alternative
• Counting people (headcount)
• Assigned responsibilities: who raises the alarm, who opens the doors, who counts
• People with special needs: injured people, wheelchair users, people who are blind
• Alerting: megaphone, bell, siren
• Exit doors must open from the inside without a key`,image:"media/images/evac_plan_adv.png"},{heading:"Running a proper drill",body:`• Alarm + evacuation + headcount + report
• Drill steps:
1. Sound the bell or siren
2. Stop all activities
3. Move in order to the nearest door
4. Stairs (do not use the elevator!)
5. Assembly point
6. Headcount and report
7. Do not go back inside until the "all clear" is given
• Record the evacuation time and reduce it with every drill
• Keep a record of drills`,image:"media/images/drill_procedure.png"}],examples:["ICS: Command + Operations + Planning + Logistics + Finance.","2 exit routes + 2 assembly points.","Record the evacuation time.","Counting people (headcount).","Bell or siren = alarm.","Stairs only!","Assign specific responsibilities.","Drill records."],tips:["Learn ICS.","Hold evacuation drills regularly.","The headcount is very important.","Assign specific responsibilities."],warnings:["Never use the elevator during an emergency! The elevator can stop between floors and you will be trapped inside. Always use the stairs!"],realCase:"At a school in Erbil, a teacher had learned ICS. When a fire broke out, he acted as commander and assigned roles: a team of trained staff to fight the small fire with extinguishers while it was safe, one team to evacuate the students and one team to count. Because everyone knew their role, the students got out quickly and in good order.",video:"media/videos/lesson_ics.mp4",game:{instruction:"In the ICS system, which statements are correct?",items:[{text:"Command makes the decisions",emoji:"👨‍✈️",correct:!0},{text:"Logistics is the main decision-maker",emoji:"📦",correct:!1},{text:"Operations carry out the work on site",emoji:"⚙️",correct:!0},{text:"Everyone makes decisions on their own",emoji:"🤷",correct:!1}]},activity:{prompt:"You are the safety officer for your class. The alarm bell has rung. There are 3 students with special needs in the class. What will you do?",image:"media/images/activity_ics.png",options:[{text:"First I help the students with special needs, we go in order toward the stairs, I count everyone at the assembly point and report to the teacher",correct:!0,feedback:"Helping those with special needs first, moving in order by the stairs, counting at the assembly point and reporting is the plan."},{text:"I run off quickly on my own",correct:!1,feedback:"As safety officer, your class depends on you: help students with special needs, keep order and count everyone at the assembly point."},{text:"I wait in the classroom",correct:!1,feedback:"When the alarm rings, stop all activities and leave in order; waiting inside wastes time and puts the class at risk."},{text:"I use the elevator",correct:!1,feedback:"Never use the elevator in an emergency: it can stop between floors and trap you. Always use the stairs."}]},quiz:[{question:"How many main sections does ICS have?",options:[{text:"5",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct! Command, Operations, Planning, Logistics, Finance/Admin."},{text:"3",correct:!1,feedback:"ICS has 5 main sections, not 3: Command, Operations, Planning, Logistics and Finance/Admin."},{text:"10",correct:!1,feedback:"ICS has only 5 main sections: Command, Operations, Planning, Logistics and Finance/Admin."}],explanation:"ICS has 5 main sections: Command, Operations, Planning, Logistics and Finance/Admin. At school, the principal is the commander, teachers are operations teams and trained students are helpers."},{question:"In a drill, what is done after reaching the assembly point?",options:[{text:"A headcount and a report",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct! So that we know no one is left inside the building."},{text:"Going straight back to the classroom",correct:!1,feedback:'No! Do not go back until the "all clear" is given.'},{text:"Every student goes home",correct:!1,feedback:"No! Everyone must be counted."}]},{question:"In an evacuation plan, how must exit doors open?",options:[{text:"From the inside, without a key",correct:!0},{text:"Only with a key kept by the principal",correct:!1},{text:"Only from the outside, by the safety team",correct:!1}],explanation:"In an emergency there is no time to look for a key: exit doors must open from the inside without a key so that everyone can get out quickly."},{question:"Smoke blocks the main exit route during an evacuation. What should a good plan already provide?",options:[{text:"An alternative route and an alternative assembly point that everyone knows",correct:!0},{text:"A key to a locked side door, kept in the principal's office",correct:!1},{text:"Permission to use the elevator when the stairs are blocked",correct:!1}],explanation:"A plan has a main and an alternative route and assembly point, practiced in drills. Exit doors open from the inside without a key, and the elevator is never used in an emergency."},{question:"A student who uses a wheelchair is in your class on the second floor. What does the evacuation plan do?",options:[{text:"Names helpers in advance and agrees how they will leave together",correct:!0},{text:"Lets the student wait until everyone else has left the building",correct:!1},{text:"Takes the student down in the elevator if there is a fire",correct:!1}],explanation:"People with special needs are part of the plan: helpers are named in advance and practice the way out with them in drills. The elevator is not used in a fire."}],steps:[{title:"Raise the alarm and stop everything",detail:"When the alarm sounds, stop all activities at once. If you discover the danger yourself, raise the alarm with the bell, the siren or your voice, and make sure 115 is called.",critical:!0},{title:"Help those who need it",detail:"Assigned helpers go with classmates who are injured, use a wheelchair or are blind. Nobody is left behind alone.",critical:!0},{title:"Leave in order by the stairs",detail:"Walk quickly and in order to the nearest safe exit and never use the elevator; if smoke or fire blocks the main route, use the alternative route.",critical:!0},{title:"Count everyone at the assembly point",detail:"The class gathers at the assembly point, the headcount is done at once and reported to the teacher or the person in command. Say who is missing and where they were last seen; do not go back to look for them."},{title:"Wait for the all clear",detail:'Do not go back inside until the "all clear" is given.'}],caseStudy:[{prompt:"During a lesson on the third floor the fire alarm rings. You are the class safety helper. When you open the classroom door, you see smoke coming up the main staircase. One classmate walks with crutches. What do you do?",options:[{text:"We take the alternative route from the plan, two students help the classmate on crutches and nobody uses the elevator",correct:!0,feedback:"Smoke on the main stairs means the alternative route; helpers for those with special needs and no elevator are part of the plan."},{text:"We go down the main stairs anyway, because they are the nearest way out",correct:!1,feedback:"Smoke on the stairs can overcome you; the plan has an alternative route for exactly this."},{text:"We take the elevator so that we do not have to pass through the smoke",correct:!1,feedback:"The elevator can stop between floors and trap you; use the alternative stairs."},{text:"We stay in the classroom and wait for someone to tell us what to do",correct:!1,feedback:"When the alarm rings, stop everything and leave in order; staying puts the class at risk."}]},{prompt:"At the assembly point you count the class: one student is missing. A classmate says he saw him go to the toilet just before the alarm. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I tell the teacher at once his name and where he was last seen, so that the person in command can tell 115; I do not go back in",correct:!0,feedback:"A quick, exact report lets the rescue team search the right place; going back in yourself puts a second person in danger."},{text:"I run back into the building to look for him in the toilets",correct:!1,feedback:"Never go back in before the all clear; report the missing student so that trained teams can search."},{text:"I think he probably went home, so I do not mention it",correct:!1,feedback:"Never assume; a missing person must be reported at once so that no one is left inside."},{text:"I wait until the drill or the emergency is over before saying anything",correct:!1,feedback:"Every minute counts for someone left inside; report at once."}]}]},{id:10,icon:"Droplets",accent:"#dc2626",title:"Bleeding and fractures",subtitle:"Advanced first aid",summary:"Severe bleeding (hemorrhage) and broken bones (fractures) are among the most dangerous injuries. The right action in the first minutes can make the difference between life and death. This lesson follows the Stop the Bleed program.",blocks:[{heading:"Severe bleeding and how to stop it",body:`Types of bleeding:
• Arterial: bright red, spurting and pulsing with the heartbeat. The most dangerous!
• Venous: dark red, flows steadily
• Capillary (from very fine blood vessels): slow, drop by drop

Stopping the bleeding (Stop the Bleed):
1. Protect yourself (put on gloves) and call 122, or ask someone else to call
2. Direct pressure: place a clean cloth on the wound and press hard with both hands without stopping
3. If the cloth becomes soaked with blood, do not remove it; place another cloth on top and keep up the pressure
4. Deep wound (in the thigh, armpit or neck): fill the wound with clean gauze or cloth (Wound Packing), then press hard. This is not for chest or abdominal wounds
5. Life-threatening bleeding from an arm or leg: apply a tourniquet early, 5–7 cm above the wound`,image:"media/images/bleeding_control.png"},{heading:"Fractures",body:`Types of fracture:
• Closed: the bone is broken but the skin is intact
• Open: the bone breaks through the skin and comes out; more dangerous!

Signs:
• Pain, swelling, bruising
• Abnormal shape of the limb, inability to move it
• A cracking sound at the moment of injury

Help:
1. Do not move the injured limb!
2. Splint: immobilize the limb in the position it is in
3. Immobilize the joints above and below the fracture
4. Open fracture: press around the wound to stop the bleeding and cover the wound with a clean cloth
5. Do not try to push the protruding bone back into place!
6. Call 122`,image:"media/images/fracture_care.png"},{heading:"The tourniquet",body:`A tourniquet is used for life-threatening limb bleeding (bleeding from an arm or leg). If blood is spurting forcefully or pressure is not enough, use it early; do not leave it until last.

Position: 5–7 cm above the wound (between the wound and the body), not over a joint. If the location of the wound is unclear, apply it as high as possible on the limb.
How:
1. Wrap the tourniquet around the limb
2. Pull the free end tight and fasten it
3. Turn the rod (Windlass) until the bleeding stops completely
4. Lock the rod in place
5. Write down the time the tourniquet was applied!

A tourniquet causes pain; this is normal. Never loosen or remove it; only medical staff remove it.`,image:"media/images/tourniquet.png"}],examples:["Arterial = bright red + spurting.","Venous = dark red + steady flow.","Firm, direct pressure = the first step.","Cloth soaked with blood? Do not remove it; put another one on top.","Deep wound: wound packing + pressure.","Tourniquet: 5–7 cm above the wound, early.","Closed versus open fracture.","Write down the time the tourniquet was applied and never loosen it."],tips:["Direct pressure is the first action.","Do not let go of the pressure!","Life-threatening bleeding from an arm or leg: tourniquet early.","Write down the time it was applied."],warnings:["Once a tourniquet has been applied, never loosen or remove it! Only medical staff remove it. Write the time of application on the tourniquet or on the casualty's forehead."],realCase:"In Sulaymaniyah, a Grade 11 student who had learned first aid saw blood spurting from his friend's wound (arterial bleeding). He immediately applied firm, direct pressure to the wound and did not let go until the ambulance arrived, while another friend called 122. In arterial bleeding, every minute counts.",video:"media/videos/lesson_bleeding.mp4",game:{instruction:"About bleeding, which statements are correct?",items:[{text:"Arterial blood: bright red and spurting",emoji:"❤️",correct:!0},{text:"Arterial blood: dark red and slow",emoji:"🔵",correct:!1},{text:"Firm, direct pressure is the first step",emoji:"✋",correct:!0},{text:"Pour water on the wound to stop the bleeding",emoji:"💧",correct:!1}]},activity:{prompt:"In the laboratory, a student's hand has been cut by glass and is bleeding heavily. The blood is spurting and bright red. What will you do?",image:"media/images/activity_bleeding.png",options:[{text:"I put on gloves, apply firm, direct pressure with a clean cloth and do not let go, and I call out for help so that the teacher calls 122 and brings the first aid kit. If the bleeding does not stop, I apply a tourniquet 5–7 cm above the wound and write down the time",correct:!0,feedback:"Firm, direct pressure comes first; if the spurting bleeding does not stop, apply a tourniquet early, 5–7 cm above the wound."},{text:"I pour water on the wound",correct:!1,feedback:"Water does not stop bleeding; firm, direct pressure with a clean cloth is the first step."},{text:"I let the bleeding stop by itself",correct:!1,feedback:"Spurting, bright red blood is arterial bleeding, the most dangerous kind, and every minute counts: press hard now."},{text:"I run away",correct:!1,feedback:"Your classmate needs help now: put on gloves, press hard on the wound and call out so that someone calls 122."}]},quiz:[{question:"Where is a tourniquet applied?",options:[{text:"5–7 cm above the wound, between the wound and the body",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct! And write down the time it was applied."},{text:"Directly on the wound itself, tied as tightly as you can",correct:!1,feedback:"The tourniquet goes 5–7 cm above the wound, between the wound and the body; direct pressure is what goes on the wound."},{text:"On the nearest joint, such as the knee or the elbow",correct:!1,feedback:"No! Not on a joint; apply it above the joint."}],explanation:"Apply a tourniquet 5–7 cm above the wound, between the wound and the body, never over a joint; if the wound's location is unclear, apply it as high as possible. Write down the time."},{question:"What does an open fracture mean?",options:[{text:"The bone breaks through the skin and comes out",correct:!0,feedback:"The bone breaks through the skin, which makes an open fracture more dangerous than a closed one."},{text:"The bone is broken but the skin is intact",correct:!1,feedback:"That is a closed fracture!"},{text:"There is pain and swelling, but the bone is not broken",correct:!1,feedback:"Pain is one sign of a fracture; an open fracture means the bone has broken through the skin."}],explanation:"In an open fracture the bone breaks through the skin and comes out, which is more dangerous than a closed fracture, where the skin is intact. Never push the bone back."},{question:"The cloth you are pressing on a wound becomes soaked with blood. What do you do?",options:[{text:"Leave it, put another cloth on top and keep pressing",correct:!0},{text:"Take it off and press again firmly with a fresh, clean, dry cloth",correct:!1},{text:"Lift it to check whether the bleeding has stopped yet",correct:!1}],explanation:"Do not remove a soaked cloth: lifting it stops the pressure on the wound. Put another cloth on top and keep up firm, direct pressure without stopping."},{question:"A friend falls from a wall. His forearm looks bent and he cannot move it, but it is not bleeding. What do you do?",options:[{text:"Keep the arm still as you found it, support it and call 122",correct:!0},{text:"Pull the arm gently straight so that the bone heals well",correct:!1},{text:"Ask him to move the arm hard to test whether it is broken",correct:!1}],explanation:"Do not move an injured limb or try to straighten it. Keep it still in the position you found it, support it and call 122."},{question:"When is a tourniquet used?",options:[{text:"Early, when bleeding from an arm or leg is life-threatening",correct:!0},{text:"Only at the very end, after a long time of direct pressure",correct:!1},{text:"On any small cut that keeps bleeding slowly for a while",correct:!1},{text:"On the neck or the chest when pressing does not help",correct:!1}],explanation:"Use a tourniquet early for life-threatening bleeding from an arm or leg, when blood spurts or pressure is not enough; do not leave it until last. It is for limbs only, not the neck or chest."}],steps:[{title:"Protect yourself and call 122",detail:"Put on gloves if you have them and call 122, or ask someone else to call while you start.",critical:!0},{title:"Press hard on the wound",detail:"Place a clean cloth or gauze on the wound and press hard with both hands without stopping. If it soaks through, do not remove it: add another on top and keep pressing.",critical:!0},{title:"Pack a deep wound",detail:"For a deep wound in the thigh, armpit or neck, fill the wound with clean gauze or cloth, then press hard. This is not for chest or abdominal wounds.",critical:!0},{title:"Tourniquet early for life-threatening limb bleeding",detail:"If blood spurts from an arm or leg or pressure is not enough: a tourniquet 5–7 cm above the wound, not over a joint, tightened until the bleeding stops, with the time written down; never loosen it.",critical:!0},{title:"Fracture: keep the limb still",detail:"Do not move the injured limb or try to push a bone back. Keep it still in the position you found it, cover an open wound with a clean cloth and call 122. Do not move a person after a fall unless the place is unsafe."}],caseStudy:[{prompt:"Outside school, a teenager has fallen from his bicycle onto broken glass. He has a deep cut on the thigh and blood is soaking his trousers fast. A teacher calls 122 and hands you gloves and the first aid kit. What do you do first?",options:[{text:"I put on gloves and press hard on the wound with gauze, using both hands, without stopping",correct:!0,feedback:"Firm, direct pressure is the first action for severe bleeding; do not stop pressing."},{text:"I lift his leg high and wait to see whether the bleeding slows",correct:!1,feedback:"Raising the limb is not taught and wastes time; press hard directly on the wound."},{text:"I wash the wound with water first so that I can see the cut",correct:!1,feedback:"Washing does not stop bleeding and costs time; press hard on the wound now."},{text:"I press on a point in his groin, far from the wound",correct:!1,feedback:"Pressure points are not taught; press directly on the wound itself."}]},{prompt:"You have been pressing hard, but the gauze is soaked and bright red blood keeps spurting out from under your hands. The kit has a tourniquet. What now?",options:[{text:"I leave the gauze, put the tourniquet 5–7 cm above the wound, between the wound and the body, tighten it until the bleeding stops and note the time",correct:!0,feedback:"Spurting blood that pressure cannot stop is life-threatening: a tourniquet early, above the wound, with the time written down."},{text:"I take off the soaked gauze and start again with fresh, clean gauze",correct:!1,feedback:"Removing soaked gauze stops the pressure; add more on top and, if the spurting continues, use the tourniquet."},{text:"I tighten the tourniquet, then loosen it every few minutes to let blood flow",correct:!1,feedback:"Never loosen a tourniquet; only medical staff remove it."},{text:"I tie the tourniquet directly on the wound so that it presses the cut",correct:!1,feedback:"The tourniquet goes 5–7 cm above the wound, between the wound and the body; direct pressure is what goes on the wound."}]}]},{id:11,icon:"ShieldAlert",accent:"#db2777",title:"International safety signs",subtitle:"GHS, NFPA and ISO",summary:"International safety signs (GHS, NFPA 704 and ISO 7010) are a worldwide language for warnings. In this lesson we learn about these systems and standards.",blocks:[{heading:"GHS: the global system",body:`GHS (Globally Harmonized System) has 9 main pictograms:
1. Explosive: exploding bomb
2. Flammable: flame
3. Oxidiser (Oxidizer): flame over circle
4. Gas under pressure (Compressed Gas): gas cylinder
5. Corrosive: drops on a hand and on metal
6. Deadly poison (Acute Toxicity): skull and crossbones
7. Irritant and minor harm (Irritant/Harmful): exclamation mark (!)
8. Serious health hazard (Health Hazard): human body with a star
9. Hazard to the environment (Environmental): fish and tree

Each pictogram is black, on a white background, inside a diamond with a red border. GHS has no pictogram for radioactive materials; that symbol is found in ISO 7010 and in the transport regulations.`,image:"media/images/ghs_all.png"},{heading:"The NFPA 704 diamond",body:`The NFPA 704 diamond is made up of 4 sections:
• Red (top) = flammability: 0–4 (0 = does not burn, 4 = extremely flammable)
• Blue (left) = health: 0–4 (0 = no hazard, 4 = deadly)
• Yellow (right) = instability: 0–4 (0 = stable, 4 = may explode)
• White (bottom) = special hazards: OX = oxidizer, W with a line through it = do not use water, SA = simple asphyxiant gas

Example: gasoline = red 3, blue 1, yellow 0`,image:"media/images/nfpa_diamond.png"},{heading:"ISO 7010 signs",body:`ISO 7010 is the international standard for safety signs:
• Prohibition: a red circle with a red diagonal line. Examples: "No entry", "No smoking"
• Warning: a yellow triangle with a black border. Examples: general danger, electricity, radioactive material
• Mandatory: a blue circle. Examples: "Wear eye protection", "Wear gloves"
• Safe condition: a green square. Examples: emergency exit, first aid, emergency telephone
• Fire equipment: a red square. Examples: fire extinguisher, fire hose`,image:"media/images/iso_signs.png"}],examples:["GHS: skull = toxic.","NFPA red = flammability.","NFPA blue = health.","NFPA yellow = instability.","NFPA white = special hazards (OX, W, SA).","ISO: red circle = prohibited.","ISO: yellow triangle = warning.","ISO: green square = safety."],tips:["Learn the 9 GHS pictograms.","Know the 4 colors of NFPA 704.","ISO: red = prohibition, yellow = warning, blue = mandatory, green = safety."],warnings:["Take signs seriously! They are there to protect your life. If you see a sign you do not recognize, do not go near it and ask specialists."],realCase:"At a factory in Zakho, a new worker had not read the NFPA diamond on a chemical tank. He tried to put out a fire with water, but the white section was marked with a W with a line through it (do not use water!). The fire grew larger. If he had recognized the sign, this incident would not have happened.",video:"media/videos/lesson_symbols.mp4",game:{instruction:"Which statements are correct? Choose the correct ones:",items:[{text:"NFPA: blue = health",emoji:"🔵",correct:!0},{text:"NFPA: red = instability",emoji:"🔴",correct:!1},{text:"ISO: green = safety",emoji:"🟢",correct:!0},{text:"NFPA: yellow = flammability",emoji:"🟡",correct:!1}]},activity:{prompt:"In the laboratory there is a tank with an NFPA diamond: red 4, blue 3, yellow 2, white OX. What does it mean, and how will you handle it?",image:"media/images/activity_symbols.png",options:[{text:"It is extremely flammable (4), very dangerous to health (3), moderately unstable (2) and an oxidizer (OX). I wear the appropriate PPE and handle it carefully; I keep it away from fire and flammable materials",correct:!0,feedback:"Red 4: extremely flammable; blue 3: very dangerous to health; yellow 2: moderately unstable; OX: oxidizer. So keep it away from fire."},{text:"It is safe",correct:!1,feedback:"These are high numbers on a 0–4 scale: red 4 means extremely flammable and blue 3 very dangerous to health."},{text:"I do not know",correct:!1,feedback:"Each color and number has a meaning you can learn; if you do not recognize a sign, keep away and ask specialists."},{text:"I pour water on it",correct:!1,feedback:"Do not guess: read the whole diamond first (a W with a line through it means no water) and ask specialists."}]},quiz:[{question:"How many main pictograms does GHS have?",options:[{text:"9",correct:!0,feedback:"GHS has 9 pictograms, from the exploding bomb and the flame to the fish and tree for environmental hazards."},{text:"4",correct:!1,feedback:"Those are the 4 colors of NFPA!"},{text:"6",correct:!1,feedback:"GHS has 9 main pictograms, not 6; learn all nine, from the exploding bomb to the fish and tree."}],explanation:"GHS has 9 main pictograms, each black on a white background inside a diamond with a red border, such as the flame, the skull and crossbones and the exclamation mark."},{question:"In ISO, what does a yellow triangle mean?",options:[{text:"Warning and danger",correct:!0,feedback:"A yellow triangle with a black border warns of danger, such as electricity or radioactive material."},{text:"Prohibition of an action",correct:!1,feedback:"That is the red circle!"},{text:"Safe condition or exit",correct:!1,feedback:"That is the green square!"}],explanation:"In ISO 7010, a yellow triangle with a black border means warning, such as general danger or electricity. A red circle means prohibition, a blue circle mandatory and a green square safe condition."},{question:"In the white section of the NFPA 704 diamond, what does a W with a line through it mean?",options:[{text:"Do not use water on this material",correct:!0},{text:"The material dissolves easily in water",correct:!1},{text:"Wear waterproof gloves when handling it",correct:!1}],explanation:"A W with a line through it means: do not use water. Read the whole diamond before you act, and if you see a sign you do not recognize, do not go near it and ask specialists."},{question:"The corridor is filling with smoke and you need the way out. Which sign do you follow?",options:[{text:"A green square showing an emergency exit",correct:!0},{text:"A red square showing a fire extinguisher",correct:!1},{text:"A blue circle showing a person with a helmet",correct:!1},{text:"A yellow triangle with an exclamation mark",correct:!1}],explanation:"In ISO 7010, green squares show safe conditions such as emergency exits. Red squares mark fire equipment, blue circles mandatory actions and yellow triangles warnings."},{question:"A bottle in the laboratory shows the GHS flame-over-circle pictogram. What is the safe choice?",options:[{text:"Keep it away from fuels and flammable materials, because it is an oxidizer",correct:!0},{text:"Store it next to the fuel cans, because the bottle itself cannot burn",correct:!1},{text:"Pour it down the sink so that it cannot cause any danger later",correct:!1}],explanation:"The flame over a circle marks an oxidizer: it can make other materials burn more strongly. Keep it away from fire and flammable materials and read the SDS."}],steps:[{title:"Read the shape and color",detail:"ISO 7010: a red circle with a line means prohibition, a yellow triangle warning, a blue circle mandatory, a green square a safe condition such as an exit and a red square fire equipment."},{title:"Read the label before you use a substance",detail:"Look for the GHS pictograms on the container and read the safety data sheet (SDS) before using any chemical."},{title:"Read the whole NFPA diamond",detail:"Red is flammability, blue health and yellow instability, each from 0 to 4; check the white section for special hazards such as a W with a line through it (do not use water)."},{title:"An unknown sign: keep away and ask",detail:"If you do not recognize a sign, do not go near it; ask a teacher or a specialist, and in danger warn others and call 115.",critical:!0}],caseStudy:[{prompt:"On a school visit to a factory, your group passes a door with a yellow triangle showing a lightning bolt and a blue circle showing safety goggles. A classmate wants to slip inside to look at the machines. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I stop him: the yellow triangle warns of electrical danger and the blue circle means eye protection is required; we stay with the guide",correct:!0,feedback:"Reading both signs correctly tells you to stay out unless the staff take you in with the right protection."},{text:"We go in quickly, because there is no red circle forbidding entry",correct:!1,feedback:"A warning sign and a mandatory sign mean danger and rules inside; never enter without permission."},{text:"We go in but promise not to touch anything",correct:!1,feedback:"The signs warn of electrical danger and require eye protection you do not have; stay with the guide."},{text:"I take a photo from the doorway so that we can look at it later",correct:!1,feedback:"Going to the doorway already takes you past the warning; stay with the group and the guide."}]},{prompt:"Later, a small fire starts near a tank whose NFPA diamond has a W with a line through it in the white section. A worker runs to unroll a water hose. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I shout to warn him that the diamond says no water, we leave with the guide and I make sure 115 is told what the sign shows",correct:!0,feedback:"A W with a line through it means water can make this fire worse; warning, leaving and passing on the sign help everyone."},{text:"I help him unroll the hose so that the water reaches the fire faster",correct:!1,feedback:"The diamond forbids water on this material; helping with the hose can make the fire grow."},{text:"I go closer to the tank to read the numbers in the colored sections",correct:!1,feedback:"Going toward a fire to read a sign is dangerous; you already know enough: no water. Get out."},{text:"I fetch a bucket of water from the washroom to help",correct:!1,feedback:"Water is the one thing the diamond forbids; warn others and get out."}]}]},{id:12,icon:"Users",accent:"#4f46e5",title:"Leadership in emergencies",subtitle:"Decision-making and team leadership",summary:"Leadership in emergencies is a special task: making decisions quickly and under pressure, leading the team and coordinating with institutions. This lesson prepares students to become safety leaders.",blocks:[{heading:"Decision-making in emergencies",body:`The OODA Loop model:
• Observe: look at the situation around you and assess it
• Orient: analyze the information and identify the hazards
• Decide: choose a quick and safe plan
• Act: carry out the plan

Principles:
• Decide quickly; an imperfect decision is better than no decision
• Gather information, but do not wait for all of it
• Keep the team informed
• Keep watching, and change the plan if needed`,image:"media/images/ooda_loop.png"},{heading:"Team leadership",body:`• Assign roles: everyone should know what they will do
• Clear communication: give short, clear instructions
• Listen: the team gives you information
• Calm: if you are afraid, the team will be afraid too
• Support: help team members and back them up

At school:
• You = the class safety officer
• Roles: raising the alarm, evacuation, headcount, first aid
• Regular practice`,image:"media/images/team_leadership.png"},{heading:"Coordination and communication",body:`In an emergency, communication is the most important thing.

Emergency numbers in the Kurdistan Region:
• 115 = Civil Defense (fire, rescue, disasters)
• 122 = Ambulance (medical emergencies)
• 104 = Police

Alerting:
• Megaphone / siren
• Phones and messaging groups
• Radio

Report (when you call 115):
• Where? Say the exact location first: area, street, a nearby landmark and the floor
• What happened?
• How many people are hurt, trapped or affected?
• Any danger: fire spreading, gas, electricity, chemicals?
• What help is needed?
• Your name and your phone number
• Do not hang up until they tell you to`,image:"media/images/communication.png"}],examples:["OODA: Observe-Orient-Decide-Act.","Assign roles.","Short, clear instructions.","Keep your voice calm.","115 fire and rescue, 122 ambulance, 104 police.","Report: what + where + how many + what is needed.","Keep watching and change the plan if needed.","Communication and alerting = life."],tips:["Learn the OODA Loop.","Assign roles.","Stay calm.","Clear communication."],warnings:["Leadership in an emergency does not mean putting yourself in danger! It means leading the team wisely and calmly and making the right decisions."],realCase:"At a school in Sulaymaniyah, a Grade 12 student who had learned leadership skills calmly led her class when an earthquake struck: during the shaking everyone took cover under the desks, and after the shaking stopped she assigned roles and helped the students with special needs, and everyone reached the assembly point in good order. The teacher said that her calmness helped a great deal that day.",video:"media/videos/lesson_leadership.mp4",game:{instruction:"In the OODA Loop, what is the first step?",items:[{text:"Observe",emoji:"👀",correct:!0},{text:"Act",emoji:"🏃",correct:!1},{text:"Decide",emoji:"🧠",correct:!1},{text:"Orient",emoji:"🗺️",correct:!1}]},activity:{prompt:"You are in a lesson and you hear a loud explosion from the chemistry laboratory. The teacher is not there. You are the class safety officer and there are 18 students in the class. What will you do?",image:"media/images/activity_leadership.png",options:[{text:"Using OODA: I assess the situation, assign tasks (alerting + evacuation + helping), we leave in order by the stairs toward the assembly point, I count everyone and call 115 and 122",correct:!0,feedback:"OODA in action: assess, assign roles, leave in order by the stairs, count everyone and call 115 and 122."},{text:"I run off quickly on my own",correct:!1,feedback:"As safety officer, 18 classmates depend on you; leading calmly and assigning roles gets everyone out, not just you."},{text:"I go to the chemistry laboratory",correct:!1,feedback:"Leadership does not mean putting yourself in danger; never go toward an explosion. Lead the class out and call 115 and 122."},{text:"I just wait",correct:!1,feedback:"Waiting wastes vital time; decide quickly, because an imperfect decision is better than no decision. Lead the class out."}]},quiz:[{question:"How many steps make up the OODA Loop?",options:[{text:"4: Observe, Orient, Decide, Act",correct:!0,feedback:"Observe, Orient, Decide, Act: four steps, repeated as you keep watching and change the plan if needed."},{text:"3: Observe, Decide, Act",correct:!1,feedback:"One step is missing: the OODA Loop has 4 steps, Observe, Orient, Decide and Act."},{text:"6: Plan, Observe, Orient, Decide, Act, Review",correct:!1,feedback:"The OODA Loop has only 4 steps: Observe, Orient, Decide and Act."}],explanation:"The OODA Loop has 4 steps: Observe the situation, Orient (analyze and identify hazards), Decide on a quick, safe plan and Act. Then keep watching and change the plan if needed."},{question:"In an emergency, what is the most important thing?",options:[{text:"Clear and fast communication",correct:!0,feedback:"Clear, fast communication tells everyone what to do and gets the right help, starting with the exact location for 115."},{text:"Getting away from the scene on your own",correct:!1,feedback:"A leader does not run off alone; leadership means guiding the team calmly and keeping everyone informed."},{text:"Waiting until you have all the information",correct:!1,feedback:"Decide quickly: an imperfect decision is better than no decision, and you should not wait for all the information."}],explanation:"In an emergency, communication is the most important thing: short, clear instructions tell the team what to do, and a clear report to 115 brings the right help."},{question:"When you call 115, what do you say first?",options:[{text:"The exact location: area, street, a nearby landmark and the floor",correct:!0},{text:"Your full name, your phone number and the name of your school",correct:!1},{text:"Everything that happened, from the very beginning, in as much detail as you can",correct:!1}],explanation:"Say the exact location first, so that help reaches the right place even if the call is cut off; then say what happened, how many people are hurt, any dangers, the help needed and your name and number. Do not hang up until they tell you to."},{question:"Time is short and you are not sure your plan is perfect. What does the lesson tell a leader to do?",options:[{text:"Decide quickly: an imperfect decision is better than none",correct:!0},{text:"Wait until you have collected all the information first",correct:!1},{text:"Let the group vote on every step before anyone moves",correct:!1}],explanation:"In an emergency, decide quickly with the information you have, keep watching and change the plan if needed. Waiting for all the information or for a vote wastes vital time."},{question:"Which instruction is clearest in an emergency?",options:[{text:'"Rawa, count everyone at the gate and tell me the number."',correct:!0},{text:'"Someone should maybe count people somewhere soon."',correct:!1},{text:'"Everybody, do whatever you think is best right now."',correct:!1},{text:`"Let's all try to be careful and well organized."`,correct:!1}],explanation:"A clear instruction names the person, the task and what to report back. Vague instructions leave everyone waiting for someone else."}],steps:[{title:"Keep yourself safe first",detail:"Leadership never means walking into danger. Do not go toward a fire, an explosion or a collapse; lead from a safe place.",critical:!0},{title:"Observe and orient",detail:"Look at what is happening and identify the dangers: fire, smoke, gas, electricity and injured people."},{title:"Decide and give clear roles",detail:"Choose a quick, safe plan and give short, clear tasks by name: who raises the alarm, who helps people with special needs and who counts."},{title:"Call 115 or 122 with a clear report",detail:"Say the exact location first, then what happened, how many people are hurt or trapped, any dangers, the help needed and your name and number; do not hang up until they tell you to.",critical:!0},{title:"Keep watching and report",detail:"Count everyone, change the plan if things change and hand over to the teacher or the rescue team with a short report."}],caseStudy:[{prompt:"You are helping to lead a group of ten younger children on a picnic in a park. The adult leader has gone back to the car. A grass fire starts at the edge of the park, and the wind is pushing the smoke toward you. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I calmly gather the children, give an older child the job of keeping them together and lead everyone away from the fire and smoke, then call 115",correct:!0,feedback:"Assess, give a clear role, move the group away from the danger and call: this is the OODA Loop in action."},{text:"We try to beat out the grass fire together with branches",correct:!1,feedback:"Leadership never means walking into danger, and grass fires spread fast with the wind. Move the group away."},{text:"I tell each child to run away in whichever direction is fastest",correct:!1,feedback:"Scattered children get lost, and some may run toward the fire; keep the group together."},{text:"I wait for the adult leader to come back before doing anything",correct:!1,feedback:"An imperfect decision is better than no decision; move the group away now and call the leader and 115."}]},{prompt:"You have reached a safe open place. While you are on the phone to 115, one child is crying and two others want to run back for their bags. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I give 115 the location first, tell the group firmly that nobody goes back and ask a calm older child to comfort and count the others",correct:!0,feedback:"A clear report, a firm rule and a role for someone else let you handle the call and the group at the same time."},{text:"I hang up quickly so that I can deal with the children first",correct:!1,feedback:"Do not hang up until 115 tells you to; give someone else the job of looking after the group."},{text:"I let the two go back quickly, because the bags are not far away",correct:!1,feedback:"Nobody goes back toward a fire for things; keep the group together."},{text:"I shout at the children angrily so that they do what I say",correct:!1,feedback:"If you are afraid, the team will be afraid too; give short, firm instructions in a calm voice."}]}]},{id:13,icon:"Bomb",accent:"#b45309",title:"Mines and unexploded ordnance for teenagers",subtitle:"Curiosity, friends and social media",summary:'Mines and unexploded ordnance can stay dangerous for decades. Teenagers are curious, go out with friends and share a lot online. This lesson shows how to stay safe, how to say "no" to risky ideas and how to protect younger children.',blocks:[{heading:"Why they are still dangerous",body:"Explosive ordnance comes in many sizes, shapes and colors. It can be buried, hidden in grass, rubble or old buildings, or lying in the open. Old, rusty or broken items can still explode, and an item that has been moved once without exploding can explode the next time."},{heading:"The safety rules",points:["Do not touch, move, kick or pick it up.","Do not throw anything at it and never try to burn it.","Do not go closer to look, film or take a selfie.","Warn others and keep them away, especially younger children.","Move well away, then call 115 or tell an adult; describe the place and nearby landmarks.","Respect signs, fences and red stones; no sign does not mean safe."]},{heading:"Curiosity, friends and social media",body:'Friends may dare you to touch an object, keep one as a souvenir, pose next to a destroyed military vehicle or sell metal for pocket money. Saying "no" is not weak: it protects you and them.',points:[`Say clearly: "No. It can explode. Let's move away and tell someone."`,"If your friends do not listen, leave and tell an adult at once.","Never collect or sell military scrap: it endangers you, the buyer and the whole neighborhood.","Do not share videos or dares of people handling explosive objects; show them to an adult instead.","Do not believe or spread rumors that a place is safe; follow official announcements."]},{heading:"If you are in a dangerous area",body:`If you see a mine, a warning sign or a strange object near your feet, or there has been an explosion:
1. Stop at once and stand still; do not move your feet.
2. Call out for help and tell others not to come to you.
3. Wait for trained help, even if it takes a long time.

Never try to walk back in your footprints, test the ground or place marks. If someone is hurt, do not run to them: call for help and wait.`},{heading:"Helping younger children",points:['Teach them the simple rule: "If you did not drop it, do not pick it up."',"Show them the safe paths to school and to play, and the places to avoid.","Tell them to come to you or another adult at once if they see something strange.","Set a good example: younger children copy what older ones do."]}],examples:['A friend dares you to kick a rusty shell = say "no", move away together and tell an adult.',"Someone wants a selfie on a destroyed tank = keep away; explosive items may be in or around it.","A video of someone holding a grenade is shared in a group = do not share it; tell an adult.","A field with no signs near an old front line = not necessarily safe; use well-used paths.","A younger brother finds a colorful object = keep him away from it and tell an adult.","You see a strange object while out with friends = move well away, then call 115."],tips:["Save 115 in your phone.","When you go out with friends, stay on main roads and well-used paths.","Find out which places near your home and school are dangerous.","Talk with your family about what to do if someone finds a strange object."],warnings:["Never touch, move, collect, film up close or sell explosive ordnance or military scrap!","In a dangerous area: stop, stand still, call for help and wait; never walk back in your footprints!"],realCase:'A group of friends was walking along a road near an old army position. One of them saw a rusty object at the edge of the road and wanted to pick it up for a video. Another friend said, "No, it can explode," and they all went on along the road without touching it. At home, he told his father, who called 115 and described the place and the nearest landmark.',media:[{kind:"image",src:"media/images/w7_uxo_teens_say_no.webp",title:"Say no and move away",description:"Teenagers stop a friend from going closer to a rusty object and move away along the road."}],game:{instruction:"Which of these are safe choices?",items:[{text:'Say "no" to the dare',emoji:"🙅",correct:!0},{text:"Go closer to take a photo",emoji:"📸",correct:!1},{text:"Call 115 once you are far away",emoji:"📞",correct:!0},{text:"Sell it as scrap",emoji:"💰",correct:!1},{text:"Stay on well-used roads and paths",emoji:"🛣️",correct:!0},{text:"Throw stones at it",emoji:"🪨",correct:!1}]},activity:{prompt:"Your friends find an old shell next to a ruined building. One of them wants to post a video of himself holding it, and another says a scrap dealer would pay for it. What do you do?",options:[{text:'I say "no", we all move away without touching it, and from a safe place I call 115 or tell an adult',correct:!0,feedback:'Saying "no" and moving away protects everyone, and reporting from a safe place lets trained teams deal with the shell.'},{text:"I film it from close up as a warning to others, without touching it",correct:!1,feedback:"Going closer to film is dangerous even without touching. Warn others from far away and report it."},{text:"I let them carry it to the scrap dealer, as long as they walk slowly",correct:!1,feedback:"Carrying it can make it explode and puts the dealer and the neighbors in danger too. Leave it and report it."},{text:"I cover it with stones so that younger children do not find it",correct:!1,feedback:"Covering or marking it means staying next to the danger. Move away, keep others away and report it."}]},quiz:[{question:"A friend says an old, rusty shell is harmless. What is true?",options:[{text:"Old and rusty ordnance can still explode, even after decades",correct:!0},{text:"Rust shows that the explosive inside has already become completely harmless",correct:!1},{text:"It is harmless if someone has already moved it from its place without an explosion",correct:!1},{text:"It is harmless if it is smaller than the palm of your hand",correct:!1}],explanation:"Mines and unexploded ordnance stay dangerous for decades and can look like harmless scrap. An item that was moved once can explode the next time, and small items are dangerous too."},{question:"What is the safest way to share information about an object you saw?",options:[{text:"From far away, tell 115 or an adult the place and landmarks; do not go back for photos",correct:!0},{text:"Go back with a friend to take clear photos of it and post them in the class group",correct:!1},{text:"Post the exact place online so that other people can go there and see it for themselves",correct:!1},{text:"Wait a few days to see whether it is still there, then tell someone",correct:!1}],explanation:"Report quickly from a safe place: the place and nearby landmarks are what the team needs. Going back for photos or sending others to look puts people in danger."},{question:"You realize you may be inside a mined area. What do you do?",options:[{text:"Stop, stand still, call for help and wait",correct:!0},{text:"Walk back carefully in your own footprints to the road",correct:!1},{text:"Test the ground in front of you with a stick to find a safe path",correct:!1},{text:"Run to the nearest road as fast as you can",correct:!1}],explanation:"Standing still keeps you from stepping on another mine. Footprints are hard to see, testing the ground is a job for specialists and running is extremely dangerous."},{question:"Why should you never collect or sell military scrap?",options:[{text:"It can explode at any time and endanger you, the buyer and people nearby",correct:!0},{text:"Dealers pay much less for metal that has been lying outside in the rain for a long time",correct:!1},{text:"The metal is very heavy and can hurt your back when you carry it",correct:!1},{text:"It must be cleaned before it is sold, and cleaning takes too long",correct:!1}],explanation:"Unexploded ordnance can look like harmless scrap but remains extremely dangerous. Collecting, carrying or selling it puts you, the buyer and the whole neighborhood at risk."},{question:"Your younger cousin says he saw a strange metal object near the playground and wants to take you there to see it. What do you do?",options:[{text:"Tell him not to go back, tell an adult and call 115 with the place",correct:!0},{text:"Go with him to check whether it is really dangerous",correct:!1},{text:"Ask him to bring it home so that an adult can look at it",correct:!1},{text:"Tell him to cover it with stones so that others cannot find it",correct:!1}],explanation:"Nobody goes back to look, carries or covers a strange object. Keep children away, tell an adult and report the place and nearby landmarks to 115."}],steps:[{title:"Do not touch and do not go closer",detail:"Do not touch, move, kick or pick it up, do not throw anything at it and do not go closer to look, film or take a selfie.",critical:!0},{title:"Warn others and keep them away",detail:"Tell your friends to stop and keep everyone away, especially younger children.",critical:!0},{title:"Report it from a safe place",detail:"On a main road or a well-used path, move well away from the object, then call 115 or tell an adult. Describe the place and nearby landmarks; do not go back for photos.",critical:!0},{title:"In a dangerous area: stop, stand still, call, wait",detail:"If you see a mine, a warning sign or a strange object near your feet: stop at once, stand still, call out for help and wait for trained help, however long it takes. Never walk back in your footprints, test the ground or place marks.",critical:!0}],caseStudy:[{prompt:`You and two friends take a shortcut across a field near an old army position. There is no sign. One friend points at a rusty metal object half-buried a few steps ahead and says: "Let's dig it out for a video." What do you do?`,options:[{text:'I say "No", tell everyone to stop and stand still where they are and call 115 for help',correct:!0,feedback:"An object near your feet in a place like this may mean a mined area: stop, stand still, call for help and wait. No sign does not mean safe."},{text:"We dig it out carefully with a stick, without touching it with our hands",correct:!1,feedback:"Digging or testing the ground is a job for specialists, and it can make the object explode."},{text:"We walk back to the road in our own footprints, one behind the other",correct:!1,feedback:"Never walk back in your footprints: they are hard to see and you may step on another mine. Stand still and call for help."},{text:"I throw a stone at it from here to see whether it is dangerous",correct:!1,feedback:"Never throw anything at a suspicious object; it can explode."}]},{prompt:"You have called 115 and everyone is standing still. One friend is scared and wants to run to the road, which looks close. The other wants to put a stone next to the object so that the rescuers can find it. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I ask both to stay still and not to place anything, and we keep talking calmly and wait for trained help, however long it takes",correct:!0,feedback:"Standing still keeps you from stepping on another mine, and marking objects is for specialists; waiting calmly is the safe choice."},{text:"I let the scared friend run to the road, because it is close",correct:!1,feedback:"Running in a dangerous area is extremely dangerous, however close the road looks; stay still and wait."},{text:"I let my friend place the stone, so that the rescuers find it quickly",correct:!1,feedback:"Placing marks means moving next to the danger; describe the object to the rescuers instead."},{text:"We step carefully back into our own footprints, one at a time",correct:!1,feedback:"Never walk back in your footprints: they are hard to see. Stay still and wait for trained help."}]}]},{id:14,icon:"LifeBuoy",accent:"#0369a1",title:"Water safety for teenagers",subtitle:"Open water, friends and safe rescue",summary:"According to the World Health Organization (WHO), children and young people are among those most affected by drowning. Teenagers often swim with friends in rivers, dams and canals, where nobody may be watching and help is far away. In this lesson, you learn the hidden dangers of open water, how to refuse a dare, how to help a friend without going in and what to do if you fall in.",blocks:[{heading:"Hidden dangers of open water",body:`Rivers, dams and canals are not swimming pools:
• Currents: the water can be fast and strong, even when the surface looks calm.
• Cold water: sudden cold water makes you gasp and breathe fast, and you can breathe in water. Even strong swimmers get into difficulty.
• Banks: steep and slippery banks can make it very hard to get out.
• Depth and hidden objects: it is hard to judge how deep the water is, and rocks, metal or branches can be hidden under the surface.`},{heading:"Dares and pressure from friends",body:`A dare to jump from a height, to swim across a river or to stay in the water longer can end in drowning or a serious injury. The World Health Organization links higher drowning rates among males to riskier behavior, such as swimming alone.
• You can say no. A real friend does not push you into danger.
• Agree on the rules before you go: nobody swims alone, nobody swims after dark and nobody jumps into water of unknown depth.
• If a friend is taking a risk, speak up and tell an adult.`},{heading:"Rules for swimming and boats",body:`• Never swim alone. Swim where a lifeguard or an adult who can help is watching.
• Do not swim after dark: nobody can see you, and you cannot see the dangers.
• Never jump or dive into water when you do not know how deep it is. Hitting a rock or the bottom can injure the spinal cord and leave you paralyzed, sometimes for life.
• In a boat, always wear a life jacket, whether or not you can swim.
• Obey no-swimming signs and the instructions of Civil Defense and official announcements.`},{heading:"Helping a friend safely",body:`Do not go into the water unless you are trained and equipped for water rescue; otherwise you can drown too. Reach or throw, do not go:
1. Shout for help and call 115 and 122. Say exactly where you are.
2. Reach: from firm ground on the bank, hold out a long stick, a branch or a rope.
3. Throw: throw something that floats, such as a life ring, a ball or an empty plastic container with its lid on.
4. When the person is out of the water and is not breathing normally, start CPR: with breaths if you are trained, chest compressions only if you are not, and follow the dispatcher's instructions.
5. Everyone pulled from the water must be checked by a doctor, even if they seem well.`},{heading:"If you fall in: float to live",body:`Cold water shock makes you gasp and want to thrash around. Fight that instinct:
• Lean back, with your ears in the water and your face up.
• Spread your arms and legs, and move your hands gently to stay afloat.
• Stay calm and float until your breathing is under control.
• Then shout and signal for help, hold on to something that floats or swim to safety if you can.`}],examples:["Calm surface, strong current underneath.","Cold water: gasping and fast breathing, so float first.","Unknown depth: no jumping and no diving.","Boat: life jacket on, whether or not you can swim.","Friend in trouble: call, reach or throw, do not go.","A dare is never a reason to risk your life."],tips:["Swim only where a lifeguard or an adult who can help is watching.","Learn to swim and learn safe rescue skills with a trained instructor.","Learn CPR in a first aid course.","Save 115 and 122 in your phone and know how to describe where you are."],warnings:["Never go into the water to rescue someone unless you are trained: reach or throw, do not go.","Never jump or dive into water of unknown depth: a spinal cord injury can leave you paralyzed."],realCase:"On a hot afternoon, a group of teenagers went to a river. One of them dared the others to jump into the water from a high rock. A girl in the group said no and reminded them that nobody knew how deep the water was. One boy jumped anyway, came up gasping in the cold current and could not reach the bank. The girl did not go in. She shouted for help and called 115 and 122, while a friend held out a long branch from firm ground and another threw him an empty plastic container with its lid on. He held on to it until adults pulled him out, and he was checked at the hospital.",media:[{kind:"image",src:"media/images/w7_teen_reach_or_throw.webp",title:"Reach or throw, do not go",description:"Teenagers help a friend from the bank: one calls for help, one holds out a long branch and one throws something that floats."}],game:{instruction:"Safe or dangerous? Choose the safe choices:",items:[{text:"Swimming where a lifeguard is watching",emoji:"👀",correct:!0},{text:"Accepting a dare to swim across a river",emoji:"🌊",correct:!1},{text:"Wearing a life jacket in a boat",emoji:"🚣",correct:!0},{text:"Diving where you cannot see the bottom",emoji:"🤿",correct:!1},{text:"Swimming with friends after dark",emoji:"🌙",correct:!1},{text:"Floating on your back after falling into cold water",emoji:"🛟",correct:!0}]},activity:{prompt:"You are at a dam with friends. One friend slips from the bank into deep, cold water and is struggling to stay up. None of you is trained in water rescue. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I stay on firm ground, shout for help, call 115 and 122, and reach with a long branch or throw something that floats",correct:!0,feedback:"Calling brings trained rescuers and an ambulance, and reaching or throwing helps your friend without putting a second person in the water."},{text:"I jump in at once, because I am a strong swimmer",correct:!1,feedback:"Cold water shock and currents can overwhelm strong swimmers too, and you could drown with your friend. Call, then reach or throw; do not go."},{text:"We hold hands in a chain and walk into the water to reach our friend",correct:!1,feedback:"On a slippery bank and in deep, cold water, the whole chain can be pulled in. Stay on firm ground and reach with an object or throw something that floats."},{text:"I run to look for an adult and call nobody until I find one",correct:!1,feedback:"Every second counts. Shout for help and call 115 and 122 right away, then reach or throw from the bank."}]},quiz:[{question:"Why is it dangerous to jump into a river or dam when you do not know how deep it is?",options:[{text:"Hidden rocks or shallow places can cause a spinal cord injury",correct:!0},{text:"It is only dangerous for people who cannot swim well",correct:!1},{text:"It is only dangerous in winter, when the water in rivers is cold",correct:!1},{text:"It is only dangerous in swimming pools without a lifeguard",correct:!1}],explanation:"You cannot see what is under the water, and it is hard to judge depth. Jumping or diving into water that is too shallow is a major cause of spinal cord injury, which can leave a person paralyzed. Never jump or dive into water of unknown depth."},{question:"You fall into cold, deep water. What should you do first?",options:[{text:"Lean back and float, and control your breathing before you swim",correct:!0},{text:"Swim as hard as you can to the bank before your body gets cold",correct:!1},{text:"Hold your breath and dive down to find the bottom with your feet",correct:!1}],explanation:"Cold water shock makes you gasp and breathe fast, even if you are a strong swimmer. Float on your back until your breathing is under control; then shout for help or swim to safety."},{question:"A friend is struggling in a canal and you are not trained in water rescue. What is the safest action?",options:[{text:"Call 115 and 122, then reach with a stick or throw something that floats",correct:!0},{text:"Jump in and pull your friend out, because there is no time to call",correct:!1},{text:"Walk into the water up to your waist and hold out your hand to your friend",correct:!1},{text:"Call only your parents and wait for them to decide what to do",correct:!1}],explanation:"Reach or throw, do not go: an untrained rescuer who goes into the water can drown too. Calling 115 and 122 brings trained rescuers and an ambulance."},{question:"When should you wear a life jacket in a boat?",options:[{text:"Every time, whether or not you can swim",correct:!0},{text:"Only when the weather is stormy or windy",correct:!1},{text:"Only if you cannot swim, or if you swim badly",correct:!1},{text:"Only when the boat goes far from the bank",correct:!1}],explanation:"Life jackets reduce the risk of drowning while boating for people of all ages and swimming abilities. Wear one every time you are in a boat."},{question:"Your friend has been pulled out of the water. He coughs a lot, then says he feels fine. What is right?",options:[{text:"He still has to be checked by a doctor",correct:!0},{text:"He can go home, because he is talking normally",correct:!1},{text:"He should swim again to calm himself down",correct:!1},{text:"He only needs to drink water and sleep",correct:!1}],explanation:"Everyone pulled from the water must be checked by a doctor, even if they seem well."}],steps:[{title:"Shout for help and call 115 and 122",detail:"Shout so that others come, call 115 and 122 and say exactly where you are.",critical:!0},{title:"Reach from firm ground",detail:"Stay on firm ground on the bank and hold out a long stick, a branch or a rope.",critical:!0},{title:"Throw something that floats",detail:"If you cannot reach, throw a life ring, a ball or an empty plastic container with its lid on. Do not go into the water unless you are trained and equipped for water rescue.",critical:!0},{title:"After the rescue",detail:"If the person is not breathing normally, start CPR: with breaths if you are trained, compressions only if you are not, and follow the dispatcher. Everyone pulled from the water is checked by a doctor, even if they seem well.",critical:!0}],caseStudy:[{prompt:"On a hot day, you go with friends to a canal. No adult is there. One friend wants to dive from a small bridge, but nobody knows how deep the water is. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I say no, remind him that diving into water of unknown depth can injure the spinal cord and suggest we go where a lifeguard is watching",correct:!0,feedback:"A real friend speaks up; hidden rocks or shallow water can cause a spinal cord injury, and nobody is watching here."},{text:"I let him dive, but I watch from the bridge in case something happens",correct:!1,feedback:"Watching does not prevent the injury; nobody should dive into water of unknown depth."},{text:"I jump in first to check how deep the water is for him",correct:!1,feedback:"Jumping in to test the depth puts you at the same risk; do not go in."},{text:"I film his dive so that we can show the adults later",correct:!1,feedback:"Filming encourages the dare; say no and tell an adult."}]},{prompt:"He dives anyway, comes up gasping in the cold current and cannot reach the bank. There is a rope in your bag and an empty plastic container with its lid on nearby. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I shout for help and call 115 and 122, then from firm ground throw him the rope or the closed container",correct:!0,feedback:"Calling brings trained rescuers and an ambulance, and throwing helps him without putting a second person in the water."},{text:"I jump in after him, because I am a good swimmer",correct:!1,feedback:"Cold water and currents overwhelm strong swimmers too; reach or throw, do not go."},{text:"We form a human chain into the water to reach him",correct:!1,feedback:"The whole chain can be pulled in; stay on firm ground and throw something."},{text:"I run along the bank to find an adult before calling anyone",correct:!1,feedback:"Every second counts: shout, call 115 and 122 and throw at once."}]}]},{id:15,icon:"DoorOpen",accent:"#c2410c",title:"Smoke, fire and escape for teenagers",subtitle:"Get out, stay out and never go back",summary:"In a home fire, smoke spreads fast and kills more people than the flames themselves. Teenagers are often at home alone at night or with younger brothers and sisters. In this lesson, you learn how to get out fast, why closed doors save lives, what to do if your way out is blocked and why you must never go back inside.",blocks:[{heading:"Why smoke is so dangerous",body:`• Smoke is hot and poisonous and rises, so the air near the floor is cleaner.
• Smoke can make you pass out or confused before you even see the fire.
• In thick black smoke you cannot see anything, not even in your own room.
• A small fire can fill a whole house in a very short time, so you have little time to get out.`},{heading:"Before a fire: your escape plan",body:`• Know two ways out of every room if possible: the door and a window.
• Agree with your family on a meeting place outside, such as a tree or a neighbor's gate.
• A smoke alarm is essential. Help the adults test it every month.
• Sleep with your bedroom door closed: a closed door slows smoke and fire.
• Practice the plan with your family and teach it to younger brothers and sisters.`},{heading:"When there is a fire: get out",body:`1. Shout "Fire!" and warn everyone at home.
2. Get low and crawl under the smoke.
3. Before you open a door, feel the door and the handle with the back of your hand. If it is hot, do not open it; use another way out.
4. Close doors behind you and use the stairs, never the elevator.
5. Go straight to the meeting place and call 115 from outside.
Your job is to get out, not to put out the fire.`},{heading:"Stay out: never go back in",body:`• Never go back in for a phone, a bag, a computer or a pet. Things can be replaced; lives cannot.
• If someone is still inside, tell the rescue teams who it is and where. They have the equipment and the training.
• Do not film and do not go near the house. Stay back to keep the way clear for firefighters.
• Go back inside only when Civil Defense says it is safe.`},{heading:"If your way out is blocked: seal and signal",body:`1. Do not go into a hallway full of smoke or fire.
2. Close the door between you and the fire.
3. Block the gaps under and around the door with clothes, towels or blankets to keep smoke out.
4. Call 115 and give your exact location: address, floor and room.
5. If it is safe, signal from a window with a light-colored cloth or your phone's light. Stay low and do not jump.`}],examples:["Smoke rises: stay close to the floor.","Hot door: do not open it; use another way.","A closed door slows smoke and fire.","Phone left inside: leave it and do not go back.","Way blocked: close the door, seal the gaps, call 115, signal at the window.","Clothes on fire: stop, drop and roll."],tips:["Know two ways out of every room and agree on a meeting place outside.","Sleep with your bedroom door closed.","If you look after younger brothers and sisters, know now how you would get them out.","Save 115 in your phone and know your exact home address."],warnings:["Never go back into a burning house for anyone or anything: smoke can kill you in a few breaths.","Never use an elevator in a fire and never open a hot door."],realCase:`One night, a teenage girl was at home with her little brother. The smoke alarm went off and smoke was coming from the kitchen. She shouted, took her brother's hand and went low toward the front door, closing the kitchen door on the way. Outside, at the neighbor's gate that was their meeting place, she called 115. Her brother cried that his phone was still inside and he wanted to go back. She held him and said, "Things can be bought again; you cannot." When the Civil Defense teams arrived, she told them everyone was out, and they stayed outside until the fire was put out.`,media:[{kind:"image",src:"media/images/w10_teen_fire_escape.webp",title:"Get out low and close the door",description:"A teenager leads a younger brother low under the smoke toward the door and closes a room door behind them."}],game:{instruction:"Safe or dangerous? Choose the safe choices:",items:[{text:"Crawling out under the smoke",emoji:"🧎",correct:!0},{text:"Going back inside to get your phone",emoji:"📱",correct:!1},{text:"Closing the door behind you",emoji:"🚪",correct:!0},{text:"Taking the elevator to get out",emoji:"🛗",correct:!1},{text:"Sealing door gaps with towels when trapped",emoji:"🧺",correct:!0},{text:"Filming the fire up close",emoji:"🎥",correct:!1}]},activity:{prompt:"At night, the smoke alarm wakes you. Your bedroom door is closed. You feel it with the back of your hand and it is hot. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I keep the door shut, block the gaps with clothes, call 115 and signal from the window",correct:!0,feedback:"A hot door means fire or hot smoke is behind it. Sealing the gaps keeps smoke out, and 115 learns where you are."},{text:"I open the door quickly and run through the hallway",correct:!1,feedback:"Opening a hot door can let hot smoke and fire rush in on you."},{text:"I hide under the bed until someone comes",correct:!1,feedback:"Rescuers cannot find you in a hiding place. Stay by the window, call and signal."},{text:"I jump from the second-floor window right away",correct:!1,feedback:"Jumping can cause serious injuries. Seal and signal, and follow the instructions of 115."}]},quiz:[{question:"Why should you crawl low in smoke?",options:[{text:"Smoke rises, so the air near the floor is cleaner",correct:!0},{text:"Fire only burns at head height and does not reach the feet",correct:!1},{text:"Crawling gets you to the door faster than walking",correct:!1},{text:"Firefighters only look for people lying on the floor",correct:!1}],explanation:"Hot, poisonous smoke rises, so the air near the floor has less smoke and you can see better."},{question:"You reach the meeting place. Your brother says the family cat is still inside. What is right?",options:[{text:"Stay outside and tell the rescue teams where the cat is",correct:!0},{text:"Run back in quickly, because the cat is near the door",correct:!1},{text:"Put a wet cloth over your mouth and go in for the cat",correct:!1},{text:"Go up to a ground-floor window and call the cat loudly",correct:!1}],explanation:"Never go back into a burning house. Rescue teams have the equipment and training; tell them who or what is still inside."},{question:"Your way out is blocked by smoke. Which order is right?",options:[{text:"Close the door, seal the gaps, call 115, signal from the window",correct:!0},{text:"Open the window and the door so air flows, then call 115",correct:!1},{text:"Run fast through the smoke before the fire grows",correct:!1},{text:"Stay on the bed, call a friend and wait",correct:!1}],explanation:"A closed door and sealed gaps keep smoke out, 115 sends rescuers to your location and signaling helps them see you."},{question:`A friend says, "The elevator is faster; let's take it down." What is right?`,options:[{text:"Use the stairs, because the elevator can stop or fill with smoke",correct:!0},{text:"The elevator is safe if the fire is on another floor",correct:!1},{text:"The elevator is safe as long as the power is still on",correct:!1},{text:"The elevator is safe for teenagers but not for young children",correct:!1}],explanation:"In a fire, an elevator can stop or open its doors on the burning floor. Always use the stairs."},{question:"Why should your bedroom door be closed at night?",options:[{text:"A closed door slows smoke and fire and gives you time to get out",correct:!0},{text:"A closed door makes the smoke alarm go off sooner",correct:!1},{text:"A closed door puts the fire out completely",correct:!1},{text:"A closed door means you do not need an escape plan",correct:!1}],explanation:"A closed door does not put out a fire, but it slows smoke and heat. That gives you more time to get out, or to seal and signal."}],steps:[{title:"Warn everyone and get low",detail:'Shout "Fire!", warn everyone and crawl under the smoke.',critical:!0},{title:"Check doors and close them behind you",detail:"Do not open a hot door. Close doors behind you and use the stairs, not the elevator.",critical:!0},{title:"Stay out and call 115",detail:"Go to the meeting place, call 115 from outside and never go back in. Tell rescuers who is still inside.",critical:!0},{title:"If trapped: seal and signal",detail:"Close the door, block the gaps with clothes, call 115 with your location, signal from the window and do not jump.",critical:!0}],caseStudy:[{prompt:"You are home alone with your little sister. The smoke alarm sounds and smoke is coming from the living room. The way to the front door is still clear. What do you do first?",options:[{text:"I take my sister's hand, we get out low and I close the living room door if it is on the way",correct:!0,feedback:"While the way is clear, getting out comes first. Closing the door slows smoke and fire."},{text:"I go to see what is burning and try to put it out with water",correct:!1,feedback:"Your job is to get out, not to fight the fire. Water is dangerous on some fires and you lose escape time."},{text:"I call my mother first to ask what to do",correct:!1,feedback:"Every second inside counts. Get out first, then call 115 and your mother from outside."},{text:"I go to my room to grab my phone and bag",correct:!1,feedback:"Collecting things eats up escape time. Things can be replaced; lives cannot."}]},{prompt:"You are both outside at the meeting place and you have called 115. Your sister cries that her favorite doll is still in her room and she wants to go back. A neighbor says he will go in for it. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I keep my sister with me, ask the neighbor not to go in and tell 115 on arrival that everyone is out",correct:!0,feedback:'Nobody goes back into a burning house for a thing. Saying "everyone is out" saves rescuers from a needless search.'},{text:"I let the neighbor go, because he is an adult and knows what he is doing",correct:!1,feedback:"Adults also collapse in smoke within a few breaths. Only equipped teams go in."},{text:"I run in myself, because I know her room well",correct:!1,feedback:"Knowing the room does not protect you from smoke. In black smoke you cannot see anything."},{text:"We go to the window to try to reach the doll from outside",correct:!1,feedback:"Going near a burning house is dangerous: smoke, heat and breaking glass. Stay at the meeting place."}]}]},{id:16,icon:"TrafficCone",accent:"#ca8a04",title:"Road safety for young pedestrians, cyclists and new drivers",subtitle:"Phones, speed, seat belts and helmets",summary:"According to the World Health Organization (WHO), road traffic injuries are a leading cause of death for children and young people. Teenagers walk to school, ride bicycles, ride in friends' cars and some start to drive. In this lesson, you learn how phones, speed, seat belts and helmets change your chances and what to do at the scene of a crash.",blocks:[{heading:"Walking: eyes and ears on the road",body:`• Cross at pedestrian crossings or traffic lights where there are some.
• Stop before crossing, look both ways and listen, and cross when the road is clear.
• Keep your phone in your pocket and take out your earphones while you cross.
• At night, wear light-colored clothes or carry a light so drivers can see you.
• Never step out from behind a parked car: the driver cannot see you.`},{heading:"Bicycles and motorcycles: a helmet every time",body:`• Every time you ride a bicycle or a motorcycle, or ride on one, wear a properly fastened helmet.
• The helmet must sit firmly on your head and not move.
• At night, use lights and reflectors.
• Keep both hands on the handlebars and do not use your phone while riding.
• Follow the traffic rules and the instructions of the traffic police.`},{heading:"In the car: seat belts and a safe driver",body:`• Fasten your seat belt on every trip, in the front and in the back, even for a short ride.
• One person, one belt: two people never share a seat belt.
• Do not ride with a driver who speeds, uses a phone, is very tired or has used alcohol or drugs. Ask for another ride and tell an adult.
• If a driver is driving dangerously, calmly ask them to slow down. It is not rude; it protects everyone.`},{heading:"New drivers: speed, phones and tiredness",body:`• Drive only when you hold a legal driving license. Age and license rules follow the instructions of the competent authorities.
• Speed makes stopping distances longer and crashes worse. Keep to the speed limit and slow down in rain and at night.
• Texting and reading while driving take your eyes off the road. Silence your phone before you drive.
• Do not drive when you are tired, and never drive after alcohol or drugs.
• Friends in the car can distract a new driver: sitting calmly helps.`},{heading:"At the scene of a crash",body:`1. Keep yourself in a safe place away from traffic.
2. Call 122 for the injured and 104 for the police, and 115 too if there is fire or someone is trapped.
3. Give the exact location: the road, nearby landmarks and the direction of travel.
4. Do not move an injured person unless there is fire or another danger.
5. Do not take off a motorcyclist's helmet.
6. Follow the dispatcher's instructions and stay with the injured person until help arrives.`}],examples:["Crossing: stop, look, listen, then cross.","Phone in hand, eyes on the screen: you cannot see the road.","Bicycle: a fastened helmet every time.","In the back seat too: belt fastened.","Speeding or tired driver: do not get in.","Crash: call 122 and 104 and do not move the injured."],tips:["Plan your route to school through the safest crossing points.","Keep your bike helmet by the door so you never forget it.","Agree with your family that you can always call them if your driver is unsafe.","Save 122, 104 and 115 in your phone."],warnings:["Never text or watch videos while crossing or driving: your eyes leave the road.","Do not drive without a legal license and do not ride with a drunk or speeding driver."],realCase:`After a party, a teenage friend with a brand-new license offered to drive four friends home. He drove fast and texted with one hand. A boy in the back said, "I'll get out here," and called his father. Another friend fastened her seat belt and calmly asked the driver to hand her his phone. He slowed down. On the way, they saw a small crash and stopped in a safe place; one of them called 122 and 104 and gave the exact location, and nobody moved the injured person.`,media:[{kind:"image",src:"media/images/w10_teen_road_safety.webp",title:"Phone away, eyes on the road",description:"A teenager stops at a pedestrian crossing and looks both ways, while another rides a bicycle wearing a helmet."}],game:{instruction:"Safe or dangerous? Choose the safe choices:",items:[{text:"Wearing a seat belt in the back seat",emoji:"🚗",correct:!0},{text:"Crossing the road while looking at your phone",emoji:"📱",correct:!1},{text:"A fastened helmet on a bicycle",emoji:"🚲",correct:!0},{text:"Riding with a driver who is speeding",emoji:"💨",correct:!1},{text:"Light-colored clothes at night",emoji:"🌙",correct:!0},{text:"Two people sharing one seat belt",emoji:"👥",correct:!1}]},activity:{prompt:"Your 17-year-old friend says he will drive you home in his father's car. You know he has no license and likes to speed. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I politely say no, ask for another ride and call an adult in my family",correct:!0,feedback:"An unlicensed driver who speeds is a serious risk. Calling an adult gets you home safely."},{text:"I go with him but wear my seat belt",correct:!1,feedback:"A seat belt matters, but it does not make an untrained, speeding driver safe. Do not get in."},{text:"I go with him and ask him to drive slowly",correct:!1,feedback:"Without a license he is neither trained nor allowed to drive. Choose a safe ride first."},{text:"I ride home at night on a bicycle with no helmet and no lights",correct:!1,feedback:"That is another risk: at night without lights drivers cannot see you, and without a helmet your head is unprotected."}]},quiz:[{question:"You are at a pedestrian crossing, talking on your phone. What is right?",options:[{text:"Pause the call, look both ways, then cross",correct:!0},{text:"Cross quickly, because drivers always stop at crossings",correct:!1},{text:"Listen to the call with one ear and the traffic with the other",correct:!1},{text:"Stop in the middle of the road and finish the call",correct:!1}],explanation:"A phone takes your attention and eyes off the road. While crossing, give all your attention to the traffic."},{question:"You sit in the back of a car for a short trip. When do you need a seat belt?",options:[{text:"Every trip, front and back, even for a short ride",correct:!0},{text:"Only on highways outside town, where speeds are high",correct:!1},{text:"Only in the front seats, because the back seats are safer",correct:!1},{text:"Only when the driver is speeding or it is raining",correct:!1}],explanation:"Crashes can happen anywhere and at any speed. An unbelted back-seat passenger can be thrown forward and injure themselves and those in front."},{question:'A friend says, "A bike helmet is only for long rides." What do you answer?',options:[{text:"Falls happen near home too, so a helmet every time",correct:!0},{text:"True, helmets are not needed on nearby streets",correct:!1},{text:"Helmets are only for small children, not teenagers",correct:!1},{text:"A helmet is good, but leave it unfastened so it is comfortable",correct:!1}],explanation:"A properly fastened helmet reduces the risk of head injury. Falls happen anywhere, and an unfastened helmet can come off in a crash."},{question:"You are in a car with friends and the new driver is speeding and texting. What is the best action?",options:[{text:"Calmly ask him to slow down and hand you the phone, and get out somewhere safe if he continues",correct:!0},{text:"Stay quiet the whole way so you do not embarrass your friend in front of the others",correct:!1},{text:"Shout at him loudly and grab the steering wheel yourself to slow the car down",correct:!1},{text:"Film him on your phone so you can show his family later what he did",correct:!1}],explanation:"A calm request alerts the driver without distracting him more. Grabbing the wheel or shouting can cause the crash."},{question:"You see a motorcycle crash. The rider is on the ground with a helmet on. What is right?",options:[{text:"Stay somewhere safe, call 122 and 104 and leave the helmet on",correct:!0},{text:"Take the helmet off quickly so he can breathe easily",correct:!1},{text:"Lift him and carry him to the roadside until help arrives",correct:!1},{text:"Give him water and ask him to stand and move his legs",correct:!1}],explanation:"After a crash, the neck or back may be injured. Moving him or removing the helmet can make it worse. Move him only if there is fire or another danger."}],steps:[{title:"Stop, look and listen",detail:"Before crossing, put the phone away, look both ways and cross when the road is clear.",critical:!0},{title:"Belt and helmet every time",detail:"Buckle up in a car; wear a fastened helmet on a bicycle or motorcycle.",critical:!0},{title:"Choose a safe driver",detail:"Do not ride with a driver who is speeding, tired, drunk or unlicensed; call an adult.",critical:!0},{title:"At a crash: safety, 122 and 104",detail:"Stay away from traffic, call 122 and 104 and do not move the injured unless there is danger.",critical:!0}],caseStudy:[{prompt:'At night you are with three friends in the car of a friend with a new license. One friend says, "Go faster!" and the driver starts to speed up. What do you do?',options:[{text:'I say calmly, "Slow down, we want to get home safe," and ask the friend to stop pushing',correct:!0,feedback:"A calm voice breaks the peer pressure and brings the driver's focus back to the road."},{text:"I laugh along so I do not stand out from the group",correct:!1,feedback:"Silence and laughter push the speed up further. Speed makes crashes worse."},{text:"I unbuckle so I can move easily if there is a crash",correct:!1,feedback:"Without a belt you are thrown in a crash. Keep it fastened at all times."},{text:"I grab the steering wheel to slow the car",correct:!1,feedback:"Someone else grabbing the wheel can send the car off the road."}]},{prompt:"The driver slows down. A little later you see a motorcycle that has hit a wall; the rider is on the ground, moving, with his helmet on. The driver wants to stop in the middle of the road. What do you do?",options:[{text:"We stop at the roadside in a safe spot, call 122 and 104 with the exact location and leave the helmet on",correct:!0,feedback:"Stopping safely prevents a second crash, 122 and 104 bring help and keeping the helmet on protects his neck."},{text:"We stop in the middle of the road and all run to him",correct:!1,feedback:"Other cars can hit you. Stop in a safe place."},{text:"We take off his helmet and lift him into the car to drive him to hospital",correct:!1,feedback:"Moving him and removing the helmet can worsen a neck or back injury. Bring 122."},{text:"We film it and drive on, because someone else will stop",correct:!1,feedback:"Maybe nobody stops. Calling 122 and 104 can save a life."}]}]},{id:17,icon:"HeartHandshake",accent:"#7c3aed",title:"Supporting a friend after a disaster",subtitle:"Look, listen and link",summary:"After a fire, flood, earthquake or accident, many young people feel fear, sadness or anger. These are normal reactions. Friends are often the first to notice that something is wrong. In this lesson, you learn how to support a friend with psychological first aid, when to tell an adult and what to do if your friend talks about hurting themselves.",blocks:[{heading:"Normal reactions after a disaster",body:`• Fear, sadness, anger or feeling guilty.
• Trouble sleeping, nightmares or thinking about what happened again and again.
• Pulling away from friends, or losing interest in games and school.
• Headaches or stomachaches with no clear cause.
Most of these reactions fade with time and support.`},{heading:"Look",body:`• First make sure the place is safe, for you and for your friend.
• Notice how your friend is: injured, very upset or alone?
• If there is an injury or urgent danger, call 122 or 115 first.`},{heading:"Listen",body:`• Come close calmly and say that you are there.
• Let your friend talk if they want to, but do not push them to tell what happened.
• Listen without judging and accept their feelings: "This is hard. You are not alone."
• Do not make promises you cannot keep, such as "Everything will be fine."`},{heading:"Link: to a trusted adult",body:`• Help your friend tell a trusted adult: a parent, a teacher, the school counselor or a doctor.
• Offer to go with them.
• If the reactions do not ease after a few weeks or get worse, professional help is needed.
• You are not a doctor or a counselor. Your job is to listen and link, not to treat.`},{heading:"If a friend talks about hurting themselves",body:`1. Take what they say seriously. Never say "You just want attention."
2. Stay with them and do not leave them alone.
3. Get an adult right away, even if they asked you not to tell. A secret that puts a life in danger must not be kept.
4. If the danger is immediate, call 122.
5. Afterward, talk to an adult about your own feelings too.`}],examples:["Your friend cannot sleep after a flood: that is a normal reaction.",`"You are not alone" is better than "Just don't think about it."`,"Listening without too many questions.",`A friend says "I don't want to live": stay with them and get an adult at once.`,"Still worse after weeks: professional help."],tips:[`Simply say "I'm here" and sit with your friend, even without talking.`,"Help your friend keep a daily routine: eating, sleeping and seeing friends.","Do not share frightening photos or videos of the event on social media.","Look after yourself too: talk to an adult about how you feel."],warnings:["Never keep a friend's secret about wanting to hurt themselves: get an adult right away.","Do not try to treat your friend yourself or give them medicine: that is the job of professionals."],realCase:`After a flood damaged his family's home, a teenage boy became quiet at school, sat alone and could not focus in class. A friend sat with him and said, "I'm here if you want to talk." A few days later, the boy said he could not sleep at night and sometimes thought life would be better without him. His friend did not leave him alone and said, "This needs help, and I'm with you." They went straight to the school counselor, who called his family and connected him with a specialist.`,media:[{kind:"image",src:"media/images/w10_teen_support_friend.webp",title:"I'm here",description:"Two teenagers sit together in a schoolyard; one listens to the other, and the school counselor can be seen in the distance."}],game:{instruction:"Helpful or not? Choose the helpful words and actions:",items:[{text:`"I'm here if you want to talk"`,emoji:"🤝",correct:!0},{text:`"Forget it, it's over"`,emoji:"🙅",correct:!1},{text:"Listening without judging",emoji:"👂",correct:!0},{text:"Keeping a secret about self-harm",emoji:"🤐",correct:!1},{text:"Going with your friend to the counselor",emoji:"🚶",correct:!0},{text:"Sharing videos of the disaster",emoji:"📲",correct:!1}]},activity:{prompt:`After an earthquake, a friend messages you: "I can't go on. Don't tell anyone." What do you do?`,options:[{text:"I reply that I am with her and tell an adult at once; if the danger is immediate, I call 122",correct:!0,feedback:"Her life matters more than the secret. Adults can bring the right help quickly."},{text:"I promise not to tell anyone so I keep her trust",correct:!1,feedback:"A secret that puts a life in danger must not be kept. Getting help matters more."},{text:`I write, "You're just tired; you'll feel better tomorrow"`,correct:!1,feedback:"That makes light of her words and may hide the danger. Take it seriously."},{text:"I wait until tomorrow to ask her myself at school",correct:!1,feedback:"The danger may be right now. Do not wait: tell an adult now."}]},quiz:[{question:"After a fire, your friend says he cannot sleep and keeps seeing what happened. What is right?",options:[{text:"This is a normal reaction; listen and help him tell an adult",correct:!0},{text:"This is a sign of serious illness and you should give him sleeping pills",correct:!1},{text:"It is best to ask him to retell every detail of what happened",correct:!1},{text:"It is best never to mention it and stay silent so he forgets",correct:!1}],explanation:"Trouble sleeping and repeated thoughts are common after a disaster. Calm listening and linking to an adult help; medicine is a doctor's job, and pushing for details is not needed."},{question:"Which phrase is best while listening?",options:[{text:`"This is hard. I'm here."`,correct:!0},{text:'"You have to be strong and not cry."',correct:!1},{text:`"It's not a big deal; others had it worse."`,correct:!1},{text:'"Everything will be totally fine, I promise."',correct:!1}],explanation:"Accepting feelings and staying close gives support. Comparing makes feelings seem small, and a promise you cannot keep breaks trust."},{question:"A friend says they want to hurt themselves and asks you not to tell anyone. What do you do first?",options:[{text:"Stay with them, get an adult at once and call 122 if the danger is immediate",correct:!0},{text:"Keep the secret and check on them by message every day",correct:!1},{text:"Tell other friends so you can all talk to them together",correct:!1},{text:"Leave them alone to calm down, then come back",correct:!1}],explanation:"A risk to life matters more than any secret. Staying with them and getting an adult are essential; friends cannot carry this risk alone."},{question:'Which of these is the "link" step?',options:[{text:"You go with your friend to the school counselor",correct:!0},{text:"You check that the place is safe before going closer",correct:!1},{text:"You listen calmly to your friend's feelings",correct:!1},{text:"You write a treatment plan for your friend yourself",correct:!1}],explanation:`"Look" is safety and needs, "listen" is calm listening and "link" is connecting your friend to an adult or a professional. Treatment is not a friend's job.`},{question:"After a week with a sad friend, you feel tired and sad yourself. What is right?",options:[{text:"Talk to an adult about your feelings and look after yourself",correct:!0},{text:"It means you are a weak friend; keep quiet",correct:!1},{text:"You must stay away from your friend completely",correct:!1},{text:"Sleep less at night so you have more time for him",correct:!1}],explanation:"Helpers need support too. Talking to an adult, sleeping and resting help you keep being a good friend."}],steps:[{title:"Look",detail:"Make sure the place is safe and see if your friend is injured or very upset; call 122 or 115 if needed.",critical:!0},{title:"Listen",detail:"Sit with your friend, let them talk as they wish and listen without judging.",critical:!1},{title:"Link",detail:"Help your friend tell a trusted adult and go with them if they want.",critical:!1},{title:"Self-harm risk: stay, get an adult, 122",detail:"If they talk about hurting themselves, do not leave them alone, get an adult at once and call 122 if the danger is immediate.",critical:!0}],caseStudy:[{prompt:"After a fire in her village, a friend sits alone at school and talks to no one. You have seen her cry several times. What do you do?",options:[{text:`I sit with her and say, "I'm here if you want to talk," without pushing`,correct:!0,feedback:"Being there without pressure opens the door; she can talk when she is ready."},{text:"I ask her to tell me right away how the fire started and what burned",correct:!1,feedback:"Pushing for details can increase fear. Let her talk when she chooses."},{text:"I leave her alone, because she will come to me if she wants",correct:!1,feedback:"People who are hurting often do not come forward. Showing you are there matters."},{text:`I tell her, "Don't cry; it was just a house"`,correct:!1,feedback:"That makes her feelings seem small. Gently accept how she feels."}]},{prompt:`A few days later she says, "Sometimes I think it would be better if I wasn't here." Then she says, "Promise you won't tell anyone." What do you do?`,options:[{text:`I say, "I care about you and I can't keep this secret," stay with her and go with her to an adult`,correct:!0,feedback:"She is not left alone, and adults can bring professional help. If the danger is immediate, call 122."},{text:"I promise and talk with her secretly every day",correct:!1,feedback:"Friends cannot carry this risk alone. Life matters more than a secret."},{text:`I say, "That's a bad thing to say; don't say it again"`,correct:!1,feedback:"Scolding silences her and hides the danger. Take it seriously and get help."},{text:"I ask friends on social media what to do",correct:!1,feedback:"That exposes her privacy and does not bring the right help. Get a trusted adult."}]}]},{id:18,icon:"Sparkles",accent:"#db2777",title:"Celebrations without injuries: fireworks and celebratory gunfire",subtitle:"Have fun safely: no burns, no bullets",summary:"Holidays, weddings and sports wins are times of joy, but fireworks can burn eyes, hands and skin, and a bullet fired into the air comes back down and can kill someone. Teenagers often want to light fireworks or see others firing guns. In this lesson, you learn how to celebrate safely, what to do if shooting starts and how to help someone who has been burned.",blocks:[{heading:"Why fireworks are dangerous",body:`• Fireworks can burn skin and clothing, injure eyes and blow off fingers.
• Even sparklers burn very hot.
• A firework that did not go off can explode when someone goes back to it.
• Homemade fireworks and those from unknown sellers are especially dangerous.
• Fireworks can set fire to dry grass, rooftops and cars.`},{heading:"How to celebrate safely",body:`• The safest choice is to watch a public display run by professionals from a safe distance.
• If an adult uses fireworks where this is allowed: one at a time, in an open area away from people, houses and dry grass.
• Never hold a firework in your hand and never point one at anyone.
• Never carry fireworks in your pocket.
• Never relight or pick up a firework that did not go off: wait, then an adult soaks it in water.
• Follow the law and the instructions of the competent authorities on fireworks.`},{heading:"Celebratory gunfire: a falling bullet kills",body:`• A bullet fired into the air comes back down and can kill or injure a person on a roof, in a courtyard or in the street, even far away.
• The shooter cannot see where the bullet falls.
• Victims are often people who were not part of the celebration, including children.
• Never hold a gun or fire one, even if someone asks you to.
• If you see a gun: do not touch it, move away and tell an adult.`},{heading:"If shooting starts",body:`1. Go indoors quickly, under a solid roof and away from windows.
2. Bring young children in from the roof and the courtyard.
3. Stay inside until the shooting stops.
4. If someone is hurt, call 122; to report the shooting, call 104.
5. Afterward, do not touch bullets or pieces on the ground; tell an adult.`},{heading:"First aid for burns and eye injuries",body:`1. Clothes on fire: stop, drop and roll.
2. Burns: cool the burn under cool running water for 20 minutes. Do not put ice, butter or toothpaste on it and do not burst blisters.
3. For large or deep burns or burns on the face, call 122.
4. Eye injury: do not rub or press the eye and do not try to remove anything stuck in it; call 122 at once.
5. Fire in a house or grass: move away and call 115.`}],examples:["A public display from a distance: the safest fun.","A dud firework: do not go back to it or pick it up.","Fireworks in a pocket can catch fire.","A bullet that goes up comes back down.","Shooting starts: go inside, away from windows.","Burn: cool running water for 20 minutes."],tips:["Celebrate with music, applause, lights and gatherings: they are safe.","Keep young children off rooftops during celebrations.","If a friend brings fireworks, calmly say no and suggest watching a public display.","Save 115, 122 and 104 in your phone."],warnings:["Never pick up or relight a firework that did not go off: it can explode in your hand.","Never handle or fire a gun: a falling bullet can kill someone far away."],realCase:'At a wedding party, some teenagers brought fireworks and a boy held one in his hand to take a photo. A girl said, "That can explode in your hand," and stopped him. Later, a firework did not go off and another boy wanted to go near it; his friends stopped him and told an adult, who waited and then soaked it in water. A little later, some guests began firing into the air. The teenagers brought the young children in from the roof and sat away from the windows until the shooting stopped.',media:[{kind:"image",src:"media/images/w10_teen_safe_celebration.webp",title:"Celebrate from a distance, without guns",description:"Teenagers and families watch a public fireworks display from a safe distance and clap."}],game:{instruction:"Safe or dangerous? Choose the safe choices:",items:[{text:"Watching a public display from afar",emoji:"🎆",correct:!0},{text:"Holding a firework for a photo",emoji:"🤳",correct:!1},{text:"Going inside when shooting starts",emoji:"🏠",correct:!0},{text:"Going back to a dud firework",emoji:"🧨",correct:!1},{text:"Applause and music instead of gunfire",emoji:"👏",correct:!0},{text:"Touching a gun left at the party",emoji:"🔫",correct:!1}]},activity:{prompt:`At a party, your friend lights a firework and it does not go off. He says, "I'll go see why it didn't work." What do you do?`,options:[{text:"I stop him, keep everyone back and tell an adult to soak it in water after waiting",correct:!0,feedback:"A dud can suddenly explode. Waiting and then soaking it in water reduces the risk."},{text:"I let him go but tell him to come back quickly",correct:!1,feedback:"Fast or not, going near a dud can burn hands and face."},{text:"I suggest he relights it with another lighter",correct:!1,feedback:"Never relight a dud: it can explode in the hand at once."},{text:"I run over at once and pour water on it myself",correct:!1,feedback:"Rushing over right away is the same risk. Wait first, and let an adult do it."}]},quiz:[{question:"Why is firing into the air at celebrations dangerous?",options:[{text:"The bullet comes back down and can kill someone far away",correct:!0},{text:"Only because the noise scares children and spoils sleep",correct:!1},{text:"Because the bullet stays in the sky and never returns",correct:!1},{text:"Only when people stand right next to the shooter",correct:!1}],explanation:"What goes up comes down. A falling bullet can still have enough force to kill, and the shooter cannot see where it lands."},{question:"During a celebration, shooting starts outside while you are on the roof with your little brother. What do you do first?",options:[{text:"Take him indoors quickly and stay away from windows",correct:!0},{text:"Stay on the roof and hide behind the low wall",correct:!1},{text:"Go to the window to see who is shooting",correct:!1},{text:"Film the shooting for social media",correct:!1}],explanation:"A falling bullet comes from above: a solid roof protects you; a low wall or a window does not."},{question:"A sparkler fell on your sister's hand and burned it. What is right?",options:[{text:"Cool it under cool running water for 20 minutes",correct:!0},{text:"Put ice directly on the burn until the pain stops",correct:!1},{text:"Spread toothpaste or butter on the burn",correct:!1},{text:"Burst the blister so the fluid drains and it heals faster",correct:!1}],explanation:"Cool running water for 20 minutes draws heat out of the skin. Ice, toothpaste and butter damage the skin, and bursting blisters raises the risk of infection."},{question:"After a party, you see a gun on a chair in the courtyard. What is right?",options:[{text:"Do not touch it, keep children away and tell an adult at once",correct:!0},{text:"Pick it up and take it back to its owner yourself",correct:!1},{text:"Check whether it is loaded, then leave it",correct:!1},{text:"Hide it somewhere so nobody sees it",correct:!1}],explanation:"A gun may be loaded and can go off suddenly. Touching, checking or moving it is for adults; your job is to stay away and tell."},{question:"Which way of celebrating is safest?",options:[{text:"Watching a professional public display from a safe distance",correct:!0},{text:"Lighting small fireworks in a crowded courtyard",correct:!1},{text:"Making fireworks at home by following videos",correct:!1},{text:"Throwing fireworks toward friends as a game",correct:!1}],explanation:"Professional displays are organized with a safe distance. Homemade fireworks and lighting them among people can cause burns and serious injuries."}],steps:[{title:"Celebrate from a distance",detail:"Watch public displays; do not hold fireworks, carry them in a pocket or point them at anyone.",critical:!0},{title:"Dud firework: stay away",detail:"Do not go back to it or relight it; tell an adult to soak it in water after waiting.",critical:!0},{title:"Shooting: go inside",detail:"Stay under a solid roof and away from windows, bring children in and never touch a gun.",critical:!0},{title:"Burns: cool water and 122",detail:"Cool the burn under cool running water for 20 minutes; call 122 for large, deep or facial burns and eye injuries.",critical:!0}],caseStudy:[{prompt:`At a wedding, a 15-year-old boy has brought a bag of fireworks and says, "Let's light them here in the courtyard." The courtyard is full of people and children. What do you do?`,options:[{text:"I say no, tell an adult at the party and suggest we watch a public display",correct:!0,feedback:"A crowded courtyard is no place for fireworks. Adults can stop it and protect the children."},{text:"I join in but only light sparklers",correct:!1,feedback:"Sparklers also burn very hot and can set clothes and skin on fire among children."},{text:"I light them myself because I am older and know how",correct:!1,feedback:"Teenagers do not light fireworks, and nobody should light them in a crowd."},{text:"I just watch from the side and say nothing",correct:!1,feedback:"Silence does not protect the children. Tell an adult before someone is hurt."}]},{prompt:"Before the adults arrive, one firework is lit and a piece lands on the hand of an 8-year-old. His hand is red and he is crying. At the same moment, someone starts firing into the air. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I take the child indoors away from windows, cool his hand under cool running water for 20 minutes and tell an adult; if the burn is large, call 122",correct:!0,feedback:"Going inside protects you both from bullets, and cool water is the best first aid for the burn."},{text:"I stay in the courtyard and put ice on his hand",correct:!1,feedback:"In the courtyard you are exposed to falling bullets, and ice damages burned skin."},{text:"I run to the shooter to take the gun away",correct:!1,feedback:"That is very dangerous. Never go near a gun; go inside and tell adults or 104."},{text:"I put toothpaste on the burn and wait outside",correct:!1,feedback:"Toothpaste does not cool a burn and can cause infection, and bullets are falling outside."}]}]}],t={lessons:e};export{t as default,e as lessons};
